"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was sent to Elmira on a 'minor theft' charge thanks to Field's clever manipulation of the law. However, Field possessed evidence of the true crime Mickles had committed and hid it in his favorite collection, intending to use it at a later date. This Field, what a cunning man... After Mickles was released, Field used these documents to coerce Mickles into doing various tasks for him without any remorse. [2192] \"For a long time, Michael has been searching for those files. His eagerness, you can imagine. He would go to the apartment whenever he had the chance. After one failure after another, he became desperate. I have no doubt that when Field found out that Michael was rummaging through his apartment day after day, he must have been laughing behind his back... On Monday night, Michael went home to sleep as he said. But on Tuesday morning, when he read the newspaper and learned that Field had been murdered, he realized it was all over. He had to search desperately for those files - if he couldn't find them, the police might find them, and then he would be finished. So, on Tuesday morning, he risked being caught by the police and returned to Field's house. Of course, the whole story about the check was just nonsense. [2193] \"But let's go back to Barry. We found a file labeled 'Miscellaneous' in the hat, detailing an unsavory matter. To abbreviate, Stephen Barry has black blood in his veins. Born into a poor family in the South, there is concrete evidence - letters, birth certificates, and so on - proving his black heritage. As we know, Field has made a business out of pursuing such things. He somehow acquired these documents, how long ago we do not know, but it must have been a long time. He had then discovered Barry's identity, finding him a struggling actor living in poverty and penniless. Field decided to leave him be for the time being. Once Barry made it big or became famous, there would be plenty of time to extort him. What Field did not anticipate was Barry's engagement to the daughter of a millionaire, the aristocratic socialite Frances Evans-Pope. It goes without saying that if Frances Evans-Pope were to find out about Barry's mixed heritage, the consequences would be dire. Furthermore, it is crucial to note that Barry had become impoverished due to gambling. His earnings had gone into the pockets of the racecourse bookmaker's agent, and he owed a significant amount of money, which he would never be able to repay with his current means, unless he married Frances. In fact, he was so destitute that he secretly urged her to marry him as soon as possible. I have always been curious about how sincerely he treated Frances. However, to be fair, I do not believe he married her solely for the money. I think he genuinely loved her - but who doesn't? But who doesn't?\" [2194] The old man smiled as he reminisced, continuing, \"Not long ago, Field brought those documents to Barry \u2013 of course, secretly. Barry gave him all the money he could, but it was barely enough to satisfy the insatiable extortionist. With no other option, he could only keep delaying Field. But Field himself was in a bind due to gambling, trying to 'recoup' funds through one small deal after another. Barry was at a dead end, realizing that unless he made Field forever silent, he would lose everything. So he planned this murder. He understood that even if he somehow managed to gather the $50,000 Field demanded \u2013 clearly an impossible task \u2013 even if he got the original documents, Field would still ruin his hopes by spreading the word. The only way out was \u2013 eliminate Field. And he did.\" [2195] \"Black blood, huh?\" Crooning grumbled, \"Poor fellow.\" [2196] \"You couldn't tell from his looks,\" said Sampson, \"he looked just as white as you and me.\" [2197] \"Barry is not like a pure black man,\" the officer countered, \"He has one drop of black blood in his veins \u2013 just one drop, but that's enough for the Evans-Pope family... Continue on. After reading these documents, we understood everything: who did it, how it was done, and the motive. But we have to make a judgment on the evidence to convict. You can't accuse someone of murder without evidence... er, what do we have? Nothing! [2198] \"Let's discuss some useful clues that could serve as evidence. The handbag of the woman - eliminate. It's worthless, you know. The source of the poison - can't find it. By the way, Barry did it according to Dr. Jones' method - the toxicologist Jones. Barry bought regular gasoline, distilled it to get tetraethyl lead. No trace left. Another possible clue - Monte Field's hat, but it's gone... Six empty tickets - we've never seen them, and the chances of seeing them are slim. The only physical evidence - those documents - only explain the motive, but can't prove anything. With this evidence, any member of Morgan or Field's gang could have committed the crime.\" [2199] \"The only hope of securing incriminating evidence lies in sending someone to sneak into Barry's apartment at night, hoping to find the hat, tickets, or other clues, such as poison or poisonous instruments. I have engaged a specialist in burglary for this purpose, who will break into Barry's apartment on Friday night while he is at the theater performing, but no such clues have been found. The hat, tickets, and poison have all been destroyed. It is clear that Barry did it, we can be sure.\" [2200] \"I gathered a group of Monday night viewers in desperation and held a meeting in the hope that someone would remember seeing Barry that night. You know, sometimes people forget things due to tension or excitement, but then they remember later. But this time it didn't work either. The only valuable testimony was from the boy selling orange juice, who saw Field pick up a handbag used at the evening party in the alley. However, this had nothing to do with Barry. Remember, on Thursday night, when we questioned the cast members, we didn't get any direct evidence either. [2201] \"So we have a perfect, hypothetical statement of fact to present to the jury, with no concrete evidence. A clever defense lawyer could easily refute our case. It's mostly indirect evidence based on reasoning. You and I both know how much of a chance we have in court... Then, my real trouble came, and Ellery was leaving town. [2202] \"I racked my brain - I was left with little. How can I convict someone without evidence? It's so frustrating. Ellery sent me a telegram with a suggestion that helped me one last time.\" [2203] \"A suggestion?\" asked Cronin. [2204] \"He suggested that I personally engage in extortion...\"\n[2205] \"You personally extort?\" Sampson looked at him. \"I don't understand.\" [2206] \"Trust Ellery, he's good at solving puzzles.\" The police officer refutes, \"I immediately understand that the only thing I can do is fabricate evidence!\" [2207] Sampson and Cronin frown in confusion. [2208] \"It's simple,\" Quine says, \"Field was killed by an unusual poison. The reason Field was killed was because he blackmailed Barry. If Barry was blackmailed in the same situation, he would use poison again - probably the same poison. My hypothesis is reasonable, right? I don't need to say that you both know, 'once bitten, twice shy.' As for Barry, if I can lure him into using tetraethyl lead on someone else, I can catch him! This poison is almost unknown - but I don't need to explain further. You can see that if I catch him with tetraethyl lead on him, that's all the evidence I need. [2209] \"How to accomplish this feat is another matter... The situation was perfect for blackmailing. In fact, I have the original documents about Barry's background and impure blood. Barry thought these documents had been destroyed - he had no reason to suspect that the ones he got from Field were carefully forged. If I blackmail him, then his situation will be the same again. So he must resort to the same tactics. [2210] \"So I took advantage of our friend Charles Michaels. I used Michaels only because, in Barry's eyes, Michaels was Field's friend, henchman, and loyal partner, and it was reasonable for him to possess the original documents. I had Michaels write a letter, dictated by me. I had Michaels write it because Barry had dealt with Field and might recognize Michaels' handwriting. This seemed like a small problem, but I couldn't take any risks. If this plan went wrong, Barry would immediately recognize it, and I would never catch him.\" [2211] \"In the letter, I attached an original document to show that this new blackmail threat was potent. I claimed that Field had given Barry a copy - the attached document proved my point. Barry didn't doubt that Michaels was imitating his master and blackmailing him. The wording was like a final ultimatum. I set a time and place, and in short, the scheme worked...\n[2212] \"I think that's all, gentlemen. Barry came, with his faithful little syringe subcutaneously injected with tetraethyl lead, and a bottle of wine - you see, except for the location, it was exactly the same as the Field case. I instructed my man - Ritter - not to take a risk. As soon as he recognized Barry, he aimed his gun at him and sounded the alarm. Fortunately, we were almost right behind them in the bushes. Barry was desperate, and if he had had even the slightest chance, he would have killed Ritter and committed suicide.\" [2213] The officer finished speaking and sighed, leaning forward to snort some snuff, and the room fell silent. [2214] Sampson shifted in his chair. \"It sounds like a thriller, Quin,\" he said admiringly, \"but there are a few things I don't understand. For example, since tetraethyl lead is unknown to people, how did Barry discover it - to the point where he could refine it himself?\" [2215] \"Oh,\" the officer smiled, \"Ever since Jones described this poison, this has bothered me. Even after catching Barry, I was still in the dark. But this shows how stupid I am - the answer has been right in front of my nose. Do you remember, when we were at the Eves-Pope estate, a doctor named Cornish was introduced? This doctor is a private friend of the old financier, and both of them are interested in medicine. In fact, I remember Ellery asking, 'Did Eves-Pope recently donate $100,000 to the Chemical Research Foundation?' Indeed, it was. One evening a few months ago, a meeting was held at the Eves-Pope house, and Barry happened to hear about tetraethyl lead. Through Cornish's introduction, a group of scientists visited this financial giant to request funding for the foundation. The evening's conversation naturally turned to rumors in medicine and recent scientific discoveries. Barry admitted that he had overheard the council member, a famous toxicologist, introducing this poison to the members. At the time, Barry didn't think he would ever use this knowledge; when he decided to kill Field, he saw the superiority of this poison and its difficult traceability.\" [2216] \"What was the significance of the message you sent me through Louis Ponzel on Thursday night, Inspector?\" Cromwell asked curiously, \"Do you remember? You asked me to observe whether Ponzel and Ponzel knew each other when they met at Louin? As I reported to you, I asked Louin, and he denied knowing Ponzel. What's going on?\" [2217] \"Ponzel,\" the policeman whispered, \"Ponzel always piques my curiosity, Tim. When I sent him to you, remember, I hadn't made any deductions about the hat... I sent him to you out of curiosity. I thought that if Louin recognized him, it might suggest a connection between Ponzel and Field. My idea wasn't confirmed, and I didn't have high hopes to begin with. Ponzel might have known Field outside, while Louin didn't know. On the other hand, I didn't want Ponzel to hang around the theater that morning; it was beneficial for both of us for him to run errands.\" [2218] \"Oh, I hope you're satisfied with the package of newspapers I sent you according to your request,\" Cromwell smiled. [2219] \"What about the anonymous letter that Morgan received? Was it a cover-up or something else?\" Sampson asked. [2220] \"It was a deceptive trick,\" Quayne said solemnly. \"Barry explained it to me last night. He heard that Morgan threatened to kill Field. Of course, he didn't know that Field was blackmailing Morgan. But he thought that if he could trick Morgan into coming to the theater on Monday night, it would create a strong false lead. If Morgan didn't come, there would be no loss. That's how he operated: he chose ordinary cheap paper, put on gloves, and printed the letter at a print shop in a messy way, signed it with a meaningless initial, and then mailed it from the post office. He was careful with his fingerprints, so he couldn't be traced from the letter. Coincidentally, Morgan fell for it and came\n[2221] Sampson nodded. \"I have one more question. How did Barry arrange for the tickets? Was it all his doing?\" [2222] \"Of course, it was. Barry convinced Field that, for fairness, the meeting and the document exchange should take place secretly in the theater. Field agreed and was easily convinced to buy eight tickets at the ticket office. He realized that, for secrecy, he needed to buy six additional tickets. He gave Barry seven and kept one for himself, which he destroyed immediately.\" [2223] The officer stood up, smiling tiredly. \"Juna! Add some coffee,\" he whispered. [2224] Sampson waved Juna away. \"Thank you, Quin, I have to go. Cromwell and I have a lot of things to deal with regarding this gang. But I can't leave until I hear the whole story from you... Quin, old friend, I truly mean it, you did an incredible job.\" [2225] \"I've never heard of such a thing,\" Cromwell said sincerely. \"A puzzle to solve, and such a perfect reasoning from start to finish!\" [2226] \"Really?\" the officer asked calmly. \"I'm glad, gentlemen. All the credit goes to Ellery. I'm so proud of my son...\"\n[2227] Sampson and Cronin left, and Juna went back to the kitchen to wash the breakfast dishes. The officer turned to the desk, picked up a pen, and quickly read the letter he had written to his son. He sighed and continued writing. [2228] Forget what I just wrote. It's been over an hour since then. Sampson and Cronin came by, and I told them all about the work we did on the case. I've never seen such a pair! They were like children, listening to a fairy tale... As I said, I know how little I've done, and how much you've done. I'm looking forward to you finding a good girl to marry, and then the Quin family will settle down and live peacefully in Italy...\n[2229] When are you coming home? Don't think I'm rushing you, but I'm so lonely, son. I... no, I mean, I'm selfish and tired. An old relic like me needs some care. You'll be home soon, won't you? Juna sends his greetings. That mischievous fellow is washing dishes in the kitchen, making my ears ring. [2230] Love, Dad\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe gripped Juno's arm and bid goodnight to Tenzel, Nelson, Wiley, and the district attorney before leaving the theater. [2051] Back at the apartment, the officer took out his key to open the door, and Juno quickly picked up a yellow envelope on the floor, obviously inserted through a gap under the door. Juno waved the letter in front of the old man. [2052] \"It's from Ellery, I bet!\" he exclaimed. \"I knew he wouldn't forget!\u201d He stood there grinning like a monkey, holding the telegram. [2053] The officer snatched the letter from Juno's hand, without taking off his hat or coat, turned on the living room light, and eagerly pulled out the yellow paper. [2054] Juno was right. [2055] Safe arrival in Shawman. Delighted with the fishing plan. I think I've solved your little problem. Join the team of famous names: Rabelais, Chaucer, Shakespeare, Dryden. They say to turn the necessity into a virtue. Why don't you try blackmail yourself? Don't yell at Juno. Love, Ellery\n[2056]The officer stared at the yellow telegram with no important content, suddenly understanding its meaning. The wrinkles on his face smoothed out. [2057]He suddenly turned to Junna, patted his messy hair under the hat, and firmly grabbed his arm. [2058]\"Junna, old friend,\" he exclaimed happily, \"let's go to the corner and have two ice cream sodas to celebrate!\" [2059]Chapter XX: Michaelis wrote a letter\n[2060]For the first time in a week, the Quinns walked into his small office in the headquarters building, throwing his coat over the chair. [2061] It was Monday morning. He rubbed his hands together, humming the tune of \"New York Sidewalks,\" and sat down at the desk with a quick and decisive reading of the voluminous letters and reports. He spent half an hour giving oral instructions, making phone calls to instruct the subordinates in the various offices of the police station, briefly studying the reports from the stenographer placed in front of him, and finally pressing one of the buttons on the table. [2062] Willie appeared immediately. [2063] \"Hello, Thomas,\" the officer said warmly, \"how are you on this beautiful autumn morning?\" [2064] Willie forced a smile. \"I'm okay, Detective,\" he said. \"How about you? You seemed unhappy last Saturday night.\" [2065] The officer chuckled. \"Let the past be past, Thomas, boy. Zhu Na and I went to the Bronx Zoo yesterday and spent four hours of happy time with our animal brothers.\" [2066] \"I bet your little troublemaker had a ball, especially with the monkeys,\" Wally gruffly says. [2067] \"All right, Thomas, don't blame John. He's a smart little egotist. He'll be a big man someday, remember my words!\" [2068] \"John?\" Wally nods seriously. \"I think you're right, officer. I'll make peace with him... What's the task for today, sir?\" [2069] \"There's a lot of tasks for today, Thomas,\" Quin said mysteriously. \"After I called you yesterday morning, did you catch Michaels?\" [2070] \"Of course, officer. He's been waiting outside for an hour. He came early, Piggott watched him. Piggott has been tailing him, making him miserable.\" [2071] \"Ah, I always say, fools make good police officers,\" Quinn chuckled. \"Bring in the lamb.\" [2072] Wellie went out for a moment and returned with the tall and fat Michael. The field's personal servant was dressed in dark clothes and looked uneasy. [2073] \"Now, Thomas, young man,\" the officer gestured for Michael to sit in the chair next to his table, \"you can go out, lock the door, and don't let the director disturb me. Understood?\" [2074] Wellie held back his curious gaze, grumbled, and left. Soon, a tall figure could be seen through the frosted glass on the door. [2075] After half an hour, a phone call summoned Wellie to his superior's office. He opened the door. On the officer's table was an unsealed cheap square envelope, with a note peeking out. Michael stood pale and trembling, with his hat crumpled in his thick hands. Wellie's sharp gaze noticed a smudge of ink on the man's left hand's fingertips. [2076] \"Take good care of Mr. Michael, Thomas,\" the officer said kindly. \"For instance, today I want you to entertain him. I'm sure you'll find something to do \u2013 I have an idea \u2013 how about watching a movie? Anyway, be nice to Michael until I tell you... don't contact anyone, Michael, heard?\" [2077] \"You know I'm honest, officer,\" Michael said displeased. \"You don't have to...\"\n[2078] \"Just precaution, Michael \u2013 just basic precautionary measures,\" the officer interrupted with a smile. \"Have fun, kids.\" [2079] The two left. Quinn sat at his desk, leaning back in his swivel chair, thoughtfully picking up the envelope in front of him. He read the cheap white paper inside and smiled. [2080] The note had no date or salutation. It began abruptly. [2081] The writer was Charles Michaels, I think you know me. For over two years, I've been Monte Field's right-hand man. [2082] I'll come straight to the point. Last Monday night, you killed Monte Field at the Romeo Theater. Monte Field told me on Sunday night that he was going to meet you at the Romeo Theater, and I was the only one who knew. [2083] There's one more thing. I know why you killed him. You killed Field to get the papers in his hat. But you didn't know that the papers in his hat weren't the originals. To prove this to you, I've attached a copy of Monte Field's testimony of Inner Johnson in the letter. If the papers you got from Monte Field's hat still exist, compare them with this one. You'll soon see that I've given you the real thing. The rest of the originals are still in my hands, in a place you'll never find. I dare say the police are looking for these papers. What do you think would happen if I walked into Officer Quinn's office with these papers and told him this story? [2084] I'll give you a chance to buy back these papers. You can bring twenty-five thousand dollars in cash to the place I specify, and then I'll give you the papers. I need money, and you need the papers and my silence. [2085] Meet me tomorrow, Tuesday at midnight, at the seventh bench on the sidewalk at the corner of 59th Street and Fifth Avenue in Central Park. I'll be wearing a gray overcoat and a gray wide-brimmed hat. Just say \"papers\" to me. [2086] This is the only way you can get these documents. Don't try to find me before the agreed time. If you don't show up, I know what I should do. [2087] The letter, with its messy handwriting and crowded letters, was signed \"Charles Michaels.\" [2088] The inspector sighed, licked the glue layer of the envelope, and sealed it. He calmly gazed at the name and address written in the same handwriting on the envelope, nonchalantly affixing a stamp in the corner. [2089] He pressed another button. The door opened, and Detective Ritter walked in. [2090] \"Good morning, Inspector.\" [2091] \"Good morning, Reiter.\" The officer thoughtfully weighed the letter in his hand. \"What have you been up to?\" [2092] Detective Reiter's feet shuffled. \"Nothing special, officer. I'm helping Officer Quin until Saturday, but I haven't received any work on the Field case this morning.\" [2093] \"Oh, I have a nice little task for you.\" The officer suddenly grinned and handed the letter over. Reiter took it with a puzzled look. \"Here you go, kid. Go to the corner of 149th Street and Third Avenue and drop this letter into the nearest mailbox!\" [2094] Reiter stared at the letter, scratched his head, looked at Quin, and finally stuffed the letter into his coat pocket. [2095] The officer leaned back in his swivel chair, satisfied, and took a pinch of snuff. [2096] Chapter XXI: Capture\n[2097] On October 2nd, Tuesday night at 11:30 PM, a tall man with a soft black hat and a black coat emerged from the lobby of a small hotel near 53rd Street on 7th Avenue. He strode quickly down 7th Avenue towards Central Park. [2098] At 59th Street, he turned east and walked along the deserted street towards 5th Avenue. When he reached the outer edge of the circular square at the entrance to Central Park, he stopped in the shadow of a large concrete pillar, leaning lazily against it. He lit a cigarette, and the light from the match illuminated his face. It was an old face with a few wrinkles, a tuft of gray-white hair sticking out from under his hat, and a small, white beard hanging from his upper lip. The match flickered out. [2099] He stood quietly against the concrete pillar, his hands in his coat pockets, smoking. If one observed closely, they would notice that his fingers were trembling slightly, and his black shoes tapped nervously on the ground. [2100] The cigarette went out, and he dropped it, glancing at his wristwatch. The hands pointed to 11:50 PM. He grumbled impatiently and walked through the park's main entrance. As he walked up the path, the arc lights around the circular square dimmed. He hesitated, not yet deciding where to go. He looked around, thought for a moment, and then sat heavily on the first bench - as if a tired man wanted to rest quietly in the darkness of the park. [2101] His head slowly drooped; his body relaxed. He seemed to be dozing off. [2102] Time passed slowly. No one passed by the black-clad man sitting on the bench. Cars roared past on 5th Avenue. The sharp whistle of traffic police occasionally cut through the cold air. The cold wind blew through the tree branches. A girl's clear laughter came from a dark corner of the park \u2013 gentle and distant, yet crystal clear. Time passed in a daze; the man slept even deeper. [2103] However, when the nearby church chimed the hour of midnight, the man's body tensed. He waited for a moment, then stood up abruptly. [2104] Instead of heading towards the entrance, he turned around and continued walking slowly and heavily down the path, his sharp and alert gaze hidden in the shadows cast by his hat and collar. He walked steadily and unhurriedly, as if counting the benches. Two \u2013 three \u2013 four \u2013 five \u2013 he stopped. In the dim darkness ahead, he could vaguely see a gray figure sitting quietly on a bench. [2105] He approached slowly. Six \u2013 seven \u2013 he didn't stop, walking straight ahead. Eight \u2013 nine \u2013 ten... Then he turned around and walked back, his steps lighter and more decisive. He approached the seventh bench quickly and stopped suddenly. Suddenly, he seemed to have made up his mind and sat down in the place where the vague figure was resting quietly. The figure grumbled and made room for the newcomer. [2106] The two sit in silence. After a while, the man in black reaches into his coat pocket and pulls out a pack of cigarettes. He lights one and holds the burning match for a moment. By the light, he sneaks a peek at the silent man sitting beside him. But the match burns out too quickly, and he can't see anything - the man sitting on the bench is wrapped up just as tightly as he is. The match goes out, and the two are once again surrounded by darkness. [2107] The man in black seems to have made up his mind. He leans forward and suddenly taps the man's knee, saying in a low, hoarse voice: \"File!\" [2108] The other man immediately wakes up. He turns half his body and examines the man beside him, muttering something that seems satisfied. He cautiously turns his body, his right hand wearing gloves reaching into the right pocket of his coat. The first man eagerly leans forward, his eyes shining. The man sitting beside him extends his hand from his pocket, tightly holding something. [2109] The owner of the hand does something remarkable. He suddenly leaps up from the bench and jumps back from the first man. At the same time, he extends his right arm towards the hunched-over, wide-eyed man in black. The arc lamp shines dimly in the distance, casting a faint light on the thing in his hand - a revolver. [2110] The first man shrieks hoarsely and jumps up like a cat. His hand darts into his coat pocket. He lunges at the man in front of him, tense and oblivious to the revolver pointing at his heart. [2111] But an incredible thing happened: the open space, the quiet scene of a countryside suddenly became tense and chaotic \u2013 rolling, roaring, and great confusion. A group of men with guns suddenly emerged from the bushes a few feet behind the bench. At the same time, another group of men with guns appeared from the other end of the path and ran towards the two men. From both ends of the path \u2013 about 100 feet from the entrance to the park \u2013 several armed police officers ran towards them. The four groups were about to converge. [2112] The man who drew the gun and jumped up from the bench fired before the reinforcements arrived. A loud bang echoed through the park, and a cluster of orange flames struck the man in black, causing him to lean forward and clutch his shoulder in pain. His knees buckled, and he collapsed to the ground, still fumbling in his pocket. [2113] But the oncoming people subdued him, and he was no longer able to do any harm. He was pinned to the ground, his hands held tightly. They remained silent until a crisp voice rang out behind them: \"Be careful, kids \u2013 watch his hand!\" [2114] Detective Richard Quinn pushed his way through the panting crowd and stood thoughtfully in front of the man rolling on the ground. \"Take his hand out, Wiley \u2013 slowly, grip it tight, or he'll stab you.\" [2115] The man on the ground struggled desperately, and Detective Thomas Wiley held his arm tightly, carefully pulling his hand out of his pocket. The hand emerged \u2013 nothing was there, and the muscles relaxed at the last moment. The two men quickly cuffed him. [2116] Willie was about to search his pockets when the officer sternly stopped him, bending down to the man rolling on the ground. [2117] The old man cautiously reached into the man's pocket, as if his life depended on it. He grabbed something, carefully pulled it out, and held it up to the light. [2118] It was a hypodermic syringe. The white liquid inside glimmered under the arc light. [2119] The officer smiled and knelt in front of the injured man, snatching off his black woolen hat. [2120] \"It's all a disguise,\" he muttered. [2121] He ripped off the person's gray beard and wiped over the wrinkled face with his hand. The person's skin immediately smeared. [2122] \"Well, well,\" the officer whispered, and the person glared at him. \"Glad to see you again, Mr. Stephen Barry, and your dear friend, tetraethyl lead.\" [2123] Chapter XXII: The Truth Unveiled\n[2124] Inspector Quine sat at the desk in the living room, hastily writing on a narrow, long note with \"Quine\" printed on the heading. [2125] It was Wednesday morning - a sunny Wednesday morning, with sunlight streaming through the skylight and the sound of the bustling street below Eighty-seventh Street faintly audible. The officer was wearing pajamas and slippers. Juana was busy clearing the table. The old man wrote:\n[2126] Dear son: When I called you last night, the case was already solved. We used Michael's name and handwriting as bait and successfully caught Stephen Barry. I should be grateful for the psychological feasibility of this plan. Barry was cornered, and like many other criminals, he thought he could pull off the same trick again and escape unscathed. [2127] I don't want to say it, but I have to tell you that I am very tired and sometimes quite dislike this job of catching people. Remembering that poor, sweet little girl, Francesca, having to face the truth that her lover was a murderer... oh, El, there is no justice in the world, nor is there any mercy. Of course, I have to bear some responsibility for her disgrace... Eves-Pope called me as soon as he heard the news, and he was very magnanimous. I think in some ways, it helped him and Francesca. [2128] The doorbell rang, and Juno quickly wiped her hands on a kitchen towel and ran to open the door. District Attorney Sampson and Timothy Cronin entered\u2014both looking excited and talking animatedly. Detective Quin covered his letter with a notebook and stood up. [2129] \"Quin, old pal!\" Sampson exclaimed, extending his hands. \"Did you see the morning paper? Congratulations!\" [2130] \"It's incredible!\" Cronin grinned, holding up a newspaper with a headline that prominently featured the capture of Stephen Barry in New York. Detective Quin's photo was prominent, and the enthusiastic report included the caption \"Quin wins again\" in two columns. [2131] However, the detective seemed quite unimpressed. He ushered the guests to sit down, ordered coffee for them, and then began discussing potential staffing changes in certain municipal departments, as if he had no interest in the case. [2132] \"Hey, hey!\" Sampson complained, \"What's wrong with you? You should be proud, Quin. It looks like you pulled a dud, not a big win.\" [2133] \"That's not it, Henry,\" the detective sighed, \"Without Ellery by my side, I seem to have no interest in anything. For heaven's sake, I wish he were here, not in that damn Maine forest!\" [2134] The two men laughed. Juno served coffee, and the detective busied himself with eating his pastry in silence. Cronin smoked, saying, \"I came here mainly to pay you a visit and express my admiration, Detective, but I'm curious about some details of this case... besides what Sampson told me on the road.\" [2135] \"I'm as confused as you are, Quin,\" the district attorney interjected, \"I think you have a story to tell us. We're all ears!\" [2136] Detective Quin smiled sadly. \"If I wanted to save face, I would say that most of the work was done by me.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[924] \"Rest in peace, Mr. Moseby,\" Ellery's laughter echoed from the foyer. He returned to the living room, waving the newspaper Michaels had dropped during his fight with Pugott. \"Really, enough, old chap, it's too much. That's your newspaper. When I picked it up, the first thing I saw was the black headline describing Field's accident, filling the front page. Have you not seen this report?\" [925] Michaels stared blankly at Ellery and the newspaper. But he lowered his eyes and muttered, \"I haven't had a chance to read the paper this morning, sir. What happened to Field?\" [926] The officer snorted. \"Field was murdered, Michaels, and you knew it all along.\" [927] \"But I truly didn't, I tell you, sir,\" the butler respectfully countered. [928] \"Don't lie to us!\" Quincannon growled. \"Tell us why you're here, or you'll have plenty of time to tell the truth once you're locked up in jail.\" [929] Michaels calmly looked at the old man. \"I'm telling the truth, sir. Mr. Field told me last night to come here this morning to pick up the check. That's all I know.\" [930] \"You were supposed to meet him here?\" [931] \"Yes, sir.\" [932] \"So why did you forget to ring the bell and use the key, as if you never expected anyone to be here, my friend?\" Quin asked. [933] \"The bell?\" The butler's eyes widened. \"I always use the key, sir. If I can open the door myself, I never disturb Mr. Field.\" [934] \"Why didn't Field give you the check yesterday?\" the officer barked. [935] \"I think he didn't have his checkbook with him, sir.\" [936] Quin raised an eyebrow. \"Your imagination isn't very rich, Michaels. When was the last time you saw Field last night?\" [937] \"Around seven o'clock, sir,\" Michaels answered promptly, \"I don't live in the apartment. It's too small, and Mr. Field values his privacy. I usually come over early to make breakfast for him, get the bathwater ready, and lay out his clothes. Then, after he goes to the office, I tidy up the room, and the rest of the time is mine until dinner. I come back at five to prepare dinner for Mr. Field. If he tells me he's going out to eat, I help him get dressed. Then my work for the evening is done... Yesterday, after I put everything away for him, he told me about the check.\" [938] \"Not a very strenuous schedule,\" Ellery murmured, \"What did you put away for Field last night, Michaels?\" [939] The man faced Ellery with respect. \"His undergarments, sir; his socks, evening shoes, crisp shirt, cufflinks, collar, white tie, tuxedo, cape, hat\u2014\"\n[940] \"Ah, yes\u2014his hat,\" Quin interjected, \"What kind of hat, Michaels?\" [941] \"The same hat he usually wears, sir,\" Michael answered, \"It's very expensive, I think, Brown Brothers brand.\" [942] Queequeg tapped the arm of his chair with his finger lazily. \"Tell me, Michael,\" he said, \"What did you do after you left here last night - around seven o'clock?\" [943] \"I went home, sir. I was so tired after packing my bags that I just ate something and went to bed - I think I went to bed around nine-thirty, sir.\" He added innocently. [944] \"Where do you live?\" Michael gave the address of 146 East 1st Street in the Bronx. \"I know... Does anyone often come to visit Field here?\" [945] Michael frowned slightly, but remained polite. \"I find it hard to say, Mr. Field is not the kind of person you think he is. And I'm not here in the evenings, so I don't know who might come after I leave. But -\"\n[946] \"But what?\" [947] \"There's a lady, sir...,\" Michaelson hesitated, looking serious. \"In this situation, I don't want to mention anyone's name\u2014\"\n[948] \"What's her name?\" [949] \"Well, sir\u2014this is a bit ungentlemanly... Lasso, Mrs. Angela Lasso, that's her name.\" Michaelson answered. [950] \"How long has Mr. Field known Mrs. Lasso?\" [951] \"A few months, sir. I believe Mr. Field met her at a gathering in Greenwich Village.\" [952] \"I see. Did they get engaged?\" [953] Michael looked a bit embarrassed. \"You could say so, sir, although their engagement was not very formal -\"\n[954] There was a pause, and the officer continued to ask, \"How long have you been employed by Mr. Field?\" [955] \"Almost three years, sir.\" [956] Quinn changed the subject, turning to a new line of questioning. He asked Michaelson about whether Field liked going to the theater, his financial situation, and his drinking habits. In these details, Michaelson confirmed what Mrs. Rask had said, but did not reveal anything new. [957] \"You said you've been working for Field for about three years now,\" Quinn said, sitting back in his chair. \"How did you get this job?\" [958] Michaelson paused before answering. \"I found it in the newspaper ad, sir.\" [959] \"Ah... since you've been working for Field for three years, Michaelson, you must know Benjamin Morgan, then?\" [960] Michaelson smiled politely. \"I certainly know Benjamin Morgan,\" he said earnestly. \"He's a very good man, sir. He's Field's partner, you know, in the legal field. But, about two years ago, they parted ways. I haven't seen Mr. Morgan since then.\" [961] \"Did you often see him before they parted?\" [962] \"No, sir.\" The sturdy butler replied with a tone of regret, \"Field is not Mr. Morgan's... type, you know, in terms of socializing. I remember seeing Mr. Morgan a few times in the apartment, but only when things were very urgent. Even then, I didn't know much, because I don't stay here at night... of course, as far as I know, Mr. Morgan hasn't come back since they dissolved the company.\" [963] Quine smiled for the first time during the conversation. \"Thank you for your honesty, Michael... I think I might become a nosy old man - do you remember if anything unpleasant happened after they parted ways?\" [964] \"Oh, no, sir!\" Michael asserted, \"I've never heard them argue or anything unpleasant. In fact, Field told me right after they dissolved the company that he and Morgan were still friends - very good friends.\" [965] Michael felt someone tap his arm and turned around politely, still with a blank expression. He found himself face to face with Ellery. \"What is it, sir?\" he respectfully asked. [966] \"Michaels, dear friend,\" Ellery said solemnly, \"I hate to bring up the past, but why haven't you told the police about your time in prison?\" [967] Michaels froze, his body stiff, unmoving, as the color drained from his face. He stared in shock at Ellery's smiling eyes, growing increasingly alarmed. [968] \"Ah\u2014ah\u2014how did you find out?\" The butler breathed heavily, his tone no longer so gentle and refined. Quinn looked approvingly at his son. Piggott and Johnson approached the trembling man. [969] Ellery lit a cigarette. \"I had no idea,\" he said cheerfully. \"Learning from the Oracle will be good for you, Michaels.\" [970] Michaels paled. He turned to Quinn, trembling. \"You\u2014you didn't ask me that, sir.\" He weakly defended himself. Then his tone became tense and bewildered again. \"And no one would want to tell the police such a thing...\"\n[971] \"Where did you serve your sentence, Michael?\" the officer asked kindly. [972] \"Emirilah Reformatory, sir,\" Michael mumbled. \"It was my first offense\u2014times were hard, I was hungry, and I stole some money\u2026 I didn't serve long, sir.\" [973] Quin stood up. \"Well, Michael, you should know that you're not a completely free man now. You can go home if you want, and you can find another job. But you have to stay where you are now and be available at any time\u2026\" He strode over to the black suitcase, opened it, and revealed a jumbled pile of clothes\u2014a dark suit, shirts, ties, socks\u2014some clean, some dirty. Quin quickly rummaged through the suitcase, closed it, and handed it to Michael. Michael stood by patiently, his face showing sadness. [974] \"I see you seem to have brought a lot of clothes, Michael,\" Quin said with a smile. \"I'm sorry, but you can't go on vacation now. That's just how life goes!\" [975] Michael murmured a \"goodbye\" and left with the suitcase. Piggott left the apartment shortly after. [976] Ellery leaned back and laughed heartily. \"What a gentleman! He's just making it up, Father... Do you think he's after something here?\" [977] \"He came here for something, obviously,\" the officer mused. \"That means there's something important here that we've overlooked... \"\n[978] He began to think when the phone rang. [979] \"Officer?\" Chief Inspector Wiley's deep voice came over the phone. \"I called the station, but you weren't there. I guess you're still at Field's... I got some interesting news from Brown Brothers' shop. Do you want me to go to Field's residence?\" [980] \"No need,\" Quinn replied. \"We're done here. I'll go to Field's law office on Money Street and then head back to the office. If anything important happens, I'll be there. Where are you now?\" [981] \"Fifth Avenue \u2013 I just left Brown Brothers.\" [982] \"Then go back to headquarters and wait for me. And, Thomas \u2013 send a police officer over right away.\" [983] Quine hung up the phone and turned to Johnson. [984] \"Stay here and wait for another officer \u2013 won't be long. Have him watch the apartment and arrange a relief, then report to headquarters... Come on, Euler. Today's going to be a busy day!\" [985] Euler's protests were in vain. His father hustled him out of the building and onto the street, where the roar of a taxi's exhaust effectively drowned out his objections. [986] Chapter 10: The Field Hat Shows Importance\n[987] At exactly 10:00 a.m., Quinn and his son pushed through the frosted-glass door with the inscription:\n[988] Montague Field\n[989] Attorney\n[990] They entered the spacious waiting room. The decor of the room, unsurprisingly, matched Field's taste in clothing. The room was empty. The inspector glanced around perplexed, then pushed open the door to the general office and stepped inside, with Ellery following behind. It was a rectangular room filled with tables, much like a newsroom, except for the rows of bookshelves stacked with thick legal tomes. [991]The room was in chaos: stenographers huddled in a small group, chattering excitedly; several male clerks whispered in corners; Hess was standing in the center of the room, engaged in conversation with a thin, pale, and melancholic man with gray hair. It was clear that Field's death had caused quite a stir in his law firm. [992]Upon seeing the Quinns enter, the employees looked at each other in surprise and began to quietly return to their desks. The room suddenly fell silent, making everyone feel awkward. Hess quickly approached them, his eyes red and weary. [993]\"Good morning, Hess,\" the detective said suddenly. \"Where is Field's private office?\" [994]The detective led them through the room to another office with the word \"Private\" written on the door. The three entered. [995]\"The guy had a thing for atmosphere, didn't he?\" Ellery chuckled, sinking into a red leather chair. [996] \"Let's hear it, Hessian,\" the officer said, taking a seat in a chair just like Ellery. [997] Hessian began to speak rapidly: \"When I arrived here last night, the door was locked. There was no light inside. I listened carefully but didn't hear any sounds. Therefore, I naturally assumed that no one was inside and spent the night in the corridor. At around eight forty-five this morning, the office manager rushed in, and I grabbed him. That's the tall one who spoke to me when you came in; his name is Oscar L\u00fcn.\" [998] \"An office manager, huh?\" the elderly man said, snorting a pinch of snuff. [999] \"Yes, sir. He either plays dumb or knows how to keep secrets well,\" Hessian continued. \"Of course, he had already seen the morning paper and was very sad about Field's death. I found that he didn't like the questions I asked... I didn't get anything out of him. He said he went straight home after work last night\u2014it seems Field left at around four o'clock and never returned\u2014he only found out about the murder from the newspaper. We've been here all morning, waiting for you to arrive.\" [1000] \"Call L\u00fcn over here.\" [1001] When Hixie returned, the tall, thin office manager followed behind him. Oscar Luin's appearance was not particularly likable. He was unusually thin, and his dark eyes looked shifty. His hooked nose and skeletal frame gave him a predatory look. The officer coolly scrutinized him. [1002] \"You're the office manager,\" he said. \"Well, what do you think about this, Luin?\" [1003] \"It's terrible, absolutely terrible,\" Luin sighed. \"I can't imagine how or why it happened. God, I was talking to him just four o'clock yesterday.\" [1004] \"Did he seem strange or worried when you were talking to Mr. Field?\" [1005] \"Not at all, sir,\" Luin answered nervously. \"In fact, he was in high spirits, joked about giants, and said