corner of the room, which had a sink, a medicine cabinet, and a small closet. Quin first checked the medicine cabinet, which contained a bottle of iodine, a bottle of hydrogen peroxide, a tube of shaving cream, and other shaving supplies. \"There's nothing here,\" Ellery said. \"What did you find in the closet?\" [1017] \"What a hateful hat!\" the officer suddenly said angrily. Just then, there was a knock on the door, and Hessey opened it, letting in a young man with a pleasant face. [1018] \"Detective Quin?\" the newcomer politely asked. [1019] \"Yes,\" the officer said sternly. \"If you're a reporter, you can report that the police will arrest the killer of Field within twenty-four hours. That's all the information I can give you right now.\" [1020] The young man smiled slightly. \"I'm sorry, Detective, but I'm not a reporter. My name is Arthur Stoez, and I'm a newbie in the District Attorney Sampson's office. The prosecutor only contacted me this morning, and I've been busy with other things - that's why I'm late. The Field incident is terrible, isn't it?\" He smiled and threw his hat and coat onto the chair. [1021] \"Everyone thinks so,\" Quin grumbled. \"He certainly caused a lot of trouble. What instructions did Sampson give you?\" [1022] \"Well, I'm not very familiar with Field's profession, which is understandable since I'm only temporarily replacing Tim Croning, who is busy with other things this morning and couldn't be reached. I started working, and Tim will take over when he finishes his current task, which will be sometime this afternoon. Croning, you know, has been pursuing Field for years. He's eager to handle these records.\" [1023] \"Good. Based on what Sampson told me about Croning\u2014if there's anything incriminating in Field's records and files, Croning will surely find it. Hess, take Stouess to the outside and introduce him to Loyn\u2014he's the office manager, Stouess. Keep a close eye on Loyn\u2014he looks like a cunning fellow. And Stouess\u2014remember, you're not looking for legal business and clients in these records, but rather for any improper content\u2026 See you later.\" [1024] Stouess smiled at him and followed Hess out. Ellery and his father stood facing each other in the room. [1025] \"What are you holding?\" the old man asked sternly. [1026] \"A book called 'What Can Handwriting Tell Us', which I just took off the shelf,\" Ellery answered lazily, \"What's wrong?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPusak stood up to squeeze past Field, and at that moment, Field was already poisoned. Pusak heard him murmur, \"Murder! Murdered!\" or something similar. [708] \"Pusak called a policeman, who then brought a doctor from the audience to check if Field was dead. The doctor confirmed that the victim had died from some kind of alcohol poisoning. Later, Assistant Coroner Plauti confirmed this, but he also added that there was an interference factor - usually, even if someone dies from alcohol poisoning, it won't happen so quickly. So, we can put aside the specific cause of death for now, as it can only be determined after autopsy. [709] \"Since there were many people in the audience and order needed to be maintained, the policeman called for help. The nearby police arrived to assist. They immediately launched an investigation. The first important question was whether the killer had the opportunity to leave the crime scene after the murder. Detective Doyle, who was the first to arrive at the crime scene, immediately ordered the theater manager to place people at every exit and both sides of the corridors to guard them. [710] \"When I arrived here, I immediately thought of this point and conducted a simple investigation myself. I circled all the exits and asked the people guarding them. I found that there was someone guarding every door in the theater during the first and second acts, of course, there were two exceptions that I will mention later. Now, through the boy who sells orange juice, Jesse Lynch, we can confirm that the victim was still alive not only during the intermission between the first and second acts - Lynch had seen him in the corridor and talked to him - but also about ten minutes after the second act began, when Lynch had delivered ginger ale to his seat, where he was later found dead. There is a usher standing under the stairs leading to the second floor in the theater, who swears that no one went up or down during the second act. This also rules out the possibility of the killer going upstairs. [711] \"The two exceptions I mentioned are the two doors at the leftmost aisle, which should have been guarded but were not, because the usher Maggie O'Connell was sitting next to her sweetheart at the time. This made me think of a possibility that the killer might have left through one of these two doors. If the killer was so talented, these two doors would be very convenient for escape. But the usher's testimony ruled out this possibility. This was something I discovered after my father asked her, and I followed up with more questions. [712] \"You snuck up on her, you little brat?\" Quinns glared at Ellery. \"I did talk to her,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"And I found an important fact that is closely related to our current investigation. O'Connell swore that she locked the door inside and out with the floor lock before leaving the two doors and sitting next to Johnny Parsen. When the commotion started, she immediately stood up from the 'priest' and ran to the door, only to find it still locked, just like when she left. So, she opened the door, and at that moment, Detective Dohal was trying to get the audience to quiet down. Unless she lied - but I don't think so. This also proves that the killer did not leave through these two doors, as the door was still locked from the inside when the body was discovered.\" [713] \"Well, I guess I'll have to change careers!\" Quinns grumbled. \"She didn't tell me anything about this, you damn woman! I'll deal with her later, this hateful woman!\" [714] \"Please be rational, Mr. Peacekeeper,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"She didn't tell you about locking the door because you didn't ask her. And she probably felt bad enough already.\" [715] \"The floor lock was a critical piece of evidence that helped us to narrow down the list of suspects to the two ushers, Maggie O'Connell and Jesse Lynch. However, the fact that the\n[716] \"Anyway, through her story, we can rule out the possibility that the killer escaped through the doors near the victim. I admit there are many possibilities - for example, Maggie O'Connell could be an accomplice. I'm just suggesting this as a possibility, not an opinion. At least in my opinion, the killer wouldn't take the risk of being seen leaving through those side doors. Moreover, leaving in an unusual way at an unusual time would be highly noticeable, especially during the second act when almost no one left. Besides, the killer couldn't have known that O'Connell would be neglecting her duties - unless she was an accomplice. Since this case was carefully planned - we must admit, the evidence suggests so - the killer might have given up the idea of escaping through the side doors.\" [717] \"The escape route, I think, is only one left, and that is the main entrance. However, the ticket taker and the doorkeeper outside confirmed that no one left the theater during the second act. Of course, except for the boy selling oranges, who was irrelevant.\" [718] \"All the exits were either guarded or locked, and from 9:35, there was someone in the alley - first Linnie, then Eleanor, then the male usher - Johnny Thurston - and then the police - these facts, along with my questioning and investigation, gentlemen. The inevitable conclusion is that the killer has been in the theater since the body was found!\" [719] After Ellery finished speaking, the room fell silent. \"By the way,\" Ellery added calmly, \"I remember asking the ushers if they saw anyone leave their seats during the second act, and they all said they couldn't remember anyone changing seats.\" [720] Quin absent-mindedly took a pinch of snuff. \"Well done - a reasonable deduction, my son. But ultimately, it's nothing surprising or decisive. Even if the killer was still in the theater, how can we catch him?\" [721] \"He didn't say you could catch him,\" said Sampson, smiling. \"Don't be so sensitive, old friend; nobody will say you were derelict in your duty during the investigation. As far as I know, you handled the whole thing very well tonight.\" [722] Quinn muttered, \"I admit, my not having thoroughly questioned the matter of the door bothers me a bit. But even if the murderer might have left immediately after the crime, I would still have asked the same question, hoping he would still be in the theater.\" [723] \"But, father\u2014of course!\" Ellery said solemnly, \"You have so many things to attend to, and my task is simply to sit back and observe, like Socrates.\" [724] \"So far, how have the people who have been surveyed been doing?\" Sampson curiously asked. [725] \"Hmm, how are they?\" Eleanor questioned, \"We can't seem to get a clear answer from either talking to them or observing their actions. We caught Parsons Johnny, the villain, but it's clear he was just a fan of the show, looking for some juicy gossip about his colleagues. As for Maggie O'Connell, she's suspicious, but we can't make any conclusions at this stage. She could be an accomplice, innocent, or just careless \u2013 she could play any role. Then there's William Pusack, who discovered Field. But have you noticed how slow-witted he is? And Benjamin Morgan \u2013 he's where we found the potential lead. But what can we make of his behavior tonight? Yes, the letter and the free ticket do sound odd, as anyone could have written that letter, even Morgan himself. I must also remember his public threat to kill Field; and the animosity between them, although the reason is unclear, has been going on for two years. Finally, there's Miss Frances Eves-Pope. Unfortunately, I wasn't present when you spoke with her. However, the fact remains that we found her handbag in the deceased's pocket \u2013 isn't that interesting? Could you explain that, please?\" [726] \"So far, that's the situation,\" Ellery continued regretfully. \"We have too many doubts from tonight's case, but too few facts.\" [727] \"So far, son,\" said Quin calmly. \"Your reasoning is flawless. But you've forgotten one important thing\u2014the empty seats. And another surprising fact\u2014the ticket stub of Field, and the only one that could possibly belong to the killer\u2014I mean the LL30 left one that Flint found\u2014these two tickets don't match. That is, the tear marks on the tickets show that the ticket taker checked these two tickets at different times!\" [728] \"Right,\" said Ellery. \"But let's put that aside for now and talk about Field's hat.\" [729] \"That hat\u2014uh, what do you think?\" Quin asked curiously. [730] \"Well, first, we're quite sure the hat didn't disappear by accident. Jesse Lynch saw the dead man's hat on his knees about ten minutes after the second act started. But now the hat is gone, and the only logical explanation is that the killer took it. Now\u2014no matter where the hat is\u2014we can immediately draw one of two conclusions: either the hat itself is connected to the case, and leaving it behind might reveal the killer's identity. We don't know what the connection is yet; or, the hat may contain something the killer wants. You might ask, why didn't the killer take what was inside and leave the hat behind? If this assumption is correct, it may be because he didn't have enough time to take it out, or he didn't know how to take it out, so he took the hat to study it later. Agree with my reasoning so far?\" [731] The district attorney nodded slowly. Quincannon sat motionless, a look of confusion in his eyes. [732] \"Now let's think about what might be in the hat. Ellery continued, wiping his glasses vigorously. \"Based on the size, shape, and volume of the hat, our range of possibilities is not very broad. What could be in a hat? Some of the things I can think of are mainly: some document, jewelry, money, or any other valuable small item that wouldn't be easily found in the hat. Obviously, this mysterious thing couldn't be in the top of the hat, because as soon as he took it off, it would fall out. So, we can conclude that whatever it is, it should be inside the hat. This immediately narrows down the possibilities. Solid large items can be ruled out. It could be jewelry, money, or documents. I think, through our understanding of Flint, we can rule out jewelry. If he carried something important, it would probably be related to his profession. [733] \"In the preliminary analysis of the missing hat, there is one more thing we need to consider. And, gentlemen, this should be crucial to our case before we solve it, that is, whether the killer knew before the murder that he had to take the hat away. In other words, did the killer know the importance of the hat, no matter how important it was? In my opinion, whether it's from the facts or from logical reasoning, it can be shown that the killer did not know the importance of the hat beforehand. [734] \"Please follow my line of thinking... Since Flint's hat is missing and no other hat was found in his place, it's clear that the hat was taken, and that's a fact. You should agree, as I pointed out earlier, that the killer is the most likely person to take the hat. Good! Let's put aside why he took the hat for now, we still have two choices: one, the killer knew beforehand that he had to take the hat; two, he didn't know beforehand that he had to take the hat. Let's examine the first possibility. If he knew beforehand, we can logically assume that the killer should have brought another hat to the theater to replace Flint's hat, instead of taking the victim's hat directly, leaving a clear clue. Bringing a replacement hat would be safer. And it shouldn't be difficult for the killer to find such a hat, because if he knew its importance beforehand, he would have further investigated the dimensions, style, and other details of Flint's hat. But we don't find a replacement hat here. We have every reason to believe that such a well-planned case should have had a replacement hat. But it's not there, so our only conclusion is that the killer didn't know the importance of the hat beforehand; otherwise, he would have taken preventive measures and left a hat. That way, the police would never know the importance of Flint's hat. [735] \"One more thing to confirm is that even if the killer had some unknown reason not to leave a replacement hat, he would have cut the hat open and\n[736] The district attorney stared at Ellery, his lips pressed together. Detective Quinn appeared to be dozing, his hand hovering between his snuff box and his nose. [737] \"What do you mean, Ellery?\" Sampson asked. \"Why is it so important that the killer didn't know the significance of the hat?\" [738] Ellery smiled. \"The murder occurred during the second act, and I wanted to use that to establish that if the killer didn't know the significance of the hat beforehand, he couldn't have planned the murder during the first intermission... Of course, Fielding's hat could appear somewhere in the theater, and once found, all these theories would be invalid. But... I don't think it will... \"\n[739] \"You've done some basic analysis, but it sounds logical,\" Sampson commended. \"You should become a lawyer.\" [740] \"You're no match for the Quinn family in terms of intellect,\" the old man suddenly broke into a grin. \"But I have to attend to something else related to the hat mystery. Ellery, have you noticed that the tailor's name is sewn onto Fielding's coat?\" [741] \"Let's do it,\" Ellery grinned, pulling out a small notebook from his coat pocket and flipping it open to a blank page. \"Brown Brothers, gentlemen\u2014that's it.\" [742] \"Alright, I'll have Wiley check it out first thing in the morning,\" Quine said. \"You must have realized that Mr. Field's clothing is of high quality. That evening suit would be worth around $300 if bought. Brown Bros. is a renowned tailor, so the price is high. Another relevant point is: all the clothes on the deceased were made by the same manufacturer. For wealthy people, this is nothing special; Brown's would make a complete outfit from head to toe to make the customer stand out. We can be more certain\u2014\"\n[743] \"Mr. Field's hat was also bought there!\" Sampson exclaimed, his face lighting up with a sense of discovery. [744] \"Absolutely correct, Tacitus,\" Quine said, grinning. \"Wiley's job is to verify the clothes, and if possible, get a replica of the same hat Mr. Field wore tonight. I'm eager to see this hat.\" [745] Sampson coughed and stood up. \"I think I should go back to bed,\" he said. \"The only reason I came here was to make sure you didn't arrest the mayor. Buddy, if my friend gets angry, my ears won't be quiet.\" [746] Quine looked up at him, smiling mockingly. \"Before you leave, Henry, tell me your position on this. I know I used high-pressure tactics tonight, but you must have realized it was necessary. Are you going to send someone from your side to assist in the investigation?\" [747] Sampson glared at him. \"When did you start feeling like I'm not satisfied with your investigation? You old fart!\" he roared. \"I've never questioned you, and I don't want to now. If you can't solve this, I don't think anyone else on my side can. My dear Quine, go ahead and do what you need to do. If you need to, you can detain half of New York, and I'll support you.\" [748] \"Thank you, Henry,\" Quine said. \"I just wanted to confirm. Now, since you're so nice, wait and see me do my thing.\" [749] He walked across the room, into the reception area, and called out to the theater from the corridor. \"Mr. Pensel, can you come here for a moment?\" [750] He came back, smiling slightly, as the dark-skinned Pensel followed behind him. [751] \"Mr. Poulson, this is District Attorney Sampson,\" Quinn introduced. They shook hands. \"Now, Mr. Poulson, you have one more thing to do before you can go rest. I want you to close the theater and not let any mice in.\" [752] Poulson's face paled. Sampson shrugged, as if he no longer cared about the matter, and Ellery nodded in agreement. [753] \"But\u2014but officer, we've been having a full house for our performances!\" the short manager moaned. \"Is it really necessary to do this?\" [754] \"Absolutely necessary, my dear fellow,\" the officer replied calmly. \"I'll have two men patrolling around the theater.\" [755] Poulson twisted his hands, casting a glance at Sampson. But the district attorney was turned away, admiring a painting on the wall. [756] \"Oh, that's terrible, Officer!\" Pincus sighed. \"Gordon Davis, the producer, will be on my case about this... But, of course\u2014if you say so, I'll do it.\" [757] \"Ah, don't be so glum, my friend,\" Quinn said, his tone more soothing. \"Through this, your theater's reputation will skyrocket. Once it reopens, you'll have to expand. Anyway, I won't keep your theater closed for too long. I'll give the necessary orders to my men outside, and you can quietly tell them what you need to do when you're done with your daily tasks tonight. You can go home after that. I'll tell you when the theater can reopen in a few days.\" [758] Pincus shook his head sadly and left after shaking hands with them. Sampson immediately turned to Quinn. \"My goodness, Quinn, that's a bit much! Why are you closing the theater? You've searched it top to bottom, haven't you?\" [759] \"But, Henry,\" Quinn said slowly, \"the hat is still missing. We've searched everyone who left the theater, and everyone has only one hat. That means the hat we're looking for must be somewhere here. If it is, I won't let anyone have the chance to come in and take it. I'll be the one to take it if it's here.\" [760] Sampson nodded. As the three of them left the office and entered the nearly empty theater, Ellery frowned with concern. Occasionally, a busy figure could be seen bending over the seats, carefully inspecting the ground. People kept coming and going from the front boxes. Chief Wiley stood by the main entrance, talking in a low voice with Pigott and Haggstrom. Detective Flint was directing some people to work in the front row of the orchestra. A group of tired janitors used vacuum cleaners to clean the theater. In a corner near the back, a plump female officer was talking to an elderly woman\u2014the same woman Pincus had referred to as Mrs. Philip. [761] Quinn and his companions approached the main entrance. Ellery and Sampson silently watched the empty theater, feeling the oppressive atmosphere. Quinn quickly spoke to Chief Inspector Wiley, whispering orders.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIt was just a matter of painting a little on a whole piece of healthy skin - I'll tell you, I was shocked. If there were a wound on the skin or if it was ingested internally, that would be another matter. So, you can imagine what would happen to Field after swallowing this stuff - he drank a lot of it!\" [1335] Ellery's eyebrows furrowed in thought. He began to wipe the lenses of his spectacles. [1336] \"This is not all,\" Dr. Jones continued. \"As far as I know \u2013 I've been working in this city for years and I have some knowledge of developments in other parts of the world \u2013 but as far as I know, tetraethyl lead has never been used for criminal purposes.\" [1337] The detective sat up straight, his eyes widening. \"That's significant, doctor!\" he murmured. \"Are you sure?\" [1338] \"Of course. That's why I'm so interested.\" [1339] \"How long would it take to kill someone with this poison, doctor?\" Ellery asked slowly. [1340] Dr. Jones looked grim. \"I can't give you an exact answer because, as far as I know, no one has died from this poison before. But I can make a fairly accurate guess. I think Field would not have lasted more than fifteen minutes, maybe twenty, after ingesting this poison.\" [1341] Quin's cough broke the ensuing silence. \"On the other hand, doctor,\" he said, \"isn't the uniqueness of this poison what makes it easy to trace? What do you think is its most common source? Where does it come from? If I wanted to use it for criminal purposes without leaving a trace, how would I get it?\" [1342] The toxicologist's face twisted into a bitter smile. \"Tracking down this poison,\" he said earnestly, \"is up to you. You can look it up. What I can confirm right now - remember, it's almost completely new to us - tetraethyl lead is most commonly found in a certain type of petroleum product. I stumbled upon the simplest method to produce it in large quantities. You won't guess how. It can be extracted from the most common and ordinary gasoline.\" [1343] The Quin father and son exclaimed in a low voice, \"Gasoline!\" The detective shouted, \"Ah - but how are we supposed to trace it?\" [1344] \"The problem is here,\" the toxicologist replied. \"I can go to a gas station on the corner, fill up a tank, drive home, and extract tetraethyl lead from the gas tank with a little time and effort.\" [1345] \"Does that mean, doctor,\" Ellery interjected hopefully, \"that the killer needs some experience with chemical experiments - familiarity with chemical analysis, or something like that?\" [1346] \"It doesn't say that. Anyone with a home distillation apparatus can extract tetraethyl lead, and leave no trace. The advantage of the process is that the boiling point of tetraethyl lead is higher than any other component in gasoline. All you have to do is extract the other components at a certain temperature, and what's left is this poison.\" [1347] The officer took a pinch of snuff and his fingers trembled slightly. \"I can only say \u2013 I'm in awe of this killer,\" he muttered. \"Tell me, Doctor, does one need to have knowledge of toxicology to know this? If he doesn't have a particular interest in this area \u2013 and hasn't been trained in it \u2013 how could he know this?\" [1348] Dr. Jones snorted. \"Officer, you surprise me. Your question has been answered already.\" [1349] \"What's going on? What do you mean?\" [1350] \"I just told you how to extract it, didn't I? If you heard this from a toxicologist, and had a distillation room, wouldn't you be able to extract some too? You don't need any other knowledge except knowing the boiling point of tetraethyl lead. Give up, Quin! You have no chance of tracing the killer with this poison. It's possible he overheard two toxicologists, or even two doctors who had heard of this poison, mention it. The rest is easy. I'm not saying it's definitely so, the killer could be a chemist. But I'm just telling you all the possibilities.\" [1351] \"I suppose the poison was put in the whiskey, right, doctor?\" Quine asked, looking bewildered. [1352] \"That's certain,\" the toxicologist replied. \"There was a large amount of whiskey components in his stomach. Of course, it's easy to poison someone this way. Anyway, most whiskeys bought now have an ether flavor. Moreover, Field may have drunk it down without realizing it was tainted - if he could realize it at all.\" [1353] \"He wouldn't taste it, would he?\" Ellery asked wearily. [1354] \"I've never tasted it, young man, so I'm not sure,\" Dr. Jones replied somewhat sharply. \"But I doubt he could taste it - at least not enough to raise his suspicions. And once he drank it, whether he realized it or not made no difference.\" [1355] Quine turned to Plaut, who was now asleep and his cigar had gone out. \"Hey, doctor!\" [1356] Plaut opened his eyes sleepily. \"Where are my slippers - I can't seem to find them, damn it!\" [1357] Despite the tense atmosphere, the assistant coroner's words made everyone laugh. When he realized what he had said, he smiled and said, \"That's just proof that I should go home and sleep, Quine. What else do you want to know?\" [1358] \"Tell me,\" Quine said, still trembling slightly, \"what did you find out from the whiskey analysis?\" [1359] \"Oh!\" Plaut immediately woke up. \"The whiskey in the bottle is just like the other whiskeys I've tested - nothing wrong with it. These years, my main job has been to test the components of the alcohol. The smell of whiskey on Field's body made me think he had drunk some cheap stuff. But the Scottish whiskey and rye whiskey you brought me from his apartment are both very high quality. They may have been purchased from the same place, in fact. They could be imported. Since the war, I haven't seen such high-quality alcohol in the country - except for those stored before the war... I think Wiley has already given you my report, and the ginger ale is also fine.\" [1360] Quine nodded. \"Well, that seems to be settled,\" he said gravely. \"We seem to have made no progress on tetraethyl lead. But just to be on the safe side, doctor - work with the professors here to see if there are any flaws in the distribution of the poison in the body. You two are very familiar with it, and it will be more effective than other people. It's just a shot in the dark, and it may not yield any results.\" [1361] \"That's fine,\" Ellery whispered. \"Every novelist has to write until the end.\" [1362] * * *\n[1363] \"I think,\" after the two left, Ellery said urgently, \"I should go to the bookstore and buy Faulkner's novel.\" [1364] \"Sit here!\" the policeman shouted, pulling him back to the chair. \"No. That damn book won't run away. I want you to sit here, I have a headache, and you're not going anywhere.\" [1365] Ellery sat on the cushion, sighing. \"Whenever I think about how all the investigations into human weaknesses are useless and a waste of time, my respected father puts such a responsibility on me again. Hey! What do you think?\" [1366] \"I'm not putting any responsibility on you,\" Quine growled, \"Stop exaggerating, I'm already dizzy. I want you to help me straighten out this mess of a case and see - uh, if we can find anything.\" [1367] \"I'm skeptical,\" Ellery said, \"Where do I start?\" [1368] \"You don't need to say anything,\" dad grumbled, \"Tonight, I'll talk, you listen. You might want to take some notes. [1369] \"Let's start with Field. First, I think we can safely assume that our friend didn't go to the theater Monday night for entertainment, but for business. Agreed?\" [1370] \"In my opinion, that's a given,\" Ellery said, \"How did Willy report Field's activities on Monday?\" [1371] \"Field went to the office at 9:30\u2014he usually gets there in the morning. He worked until noon. There were no private visitors all morning. At 12:00, he had lunch alone at the Webster Club, returned to the office at 1:30, and worked until 4:00\u2014he seemed to go straight home after work, as the doorman and elevator operator can confirm he was back in his apartment at 4:30. Michaels went to him at 5:00 and left at 6:00, and Willy found no other information. Field left the apartment at 7:30, dressed as we found him. I have a list of clients he saw that day, but it's not very useful.\" [1372] \"What's the reason for his low bank account balance?\" Ellery asked. [1373] \"As I suspected,\" Quinn answered, \"Field has been losing a lot of money in the stock market\u2014not to mention he also wastes money at the racetrack. For a sensible man, he's a gullible fool. Anyway, that explains why his personal account cash is so low. Moreover, perhaps it can more firmly indicate that the '50,000' we found on the promotional material refers to money. And this money, I'm sure, is somehow related to the person he was supposed to meet at the theater.\" [1374] \"Now, I think we can safely conclude that Field is very familiar with the killer. On the one hand, he accepted the drink offered to him without the slightest suspicion, not even asking; on the other hand, they arranged to meet in a place that seemed to be for concealment\u2014otherwise, why choose the theater?\" [1375] \"Well, I'll ask you the same question,\" Ellery interrupted him, pursing his lips, \"Why choose the theater as the meeting place? It's obvious that they were going to engage in a secret, criminal transaction. Why not the park, which is more concealed? Or the hotel lobby, which is also good? How do you answer that?\" [1376] \"Unfortunately, my son,\" the detective said mildly, \"Field didn't suspect anyone was going to kill him. In his mind, he just had to do his part in the transaction. In fact, it's likely that Field chose the theater as the meeting place himself. Perhaps he wanted to create an alibi for himself. We can't know for sure what he was trying to do. As for the hotel lobby - he would have risked being seen by others. And perhaps he didn't want to go to a secluded place like the park. Finally, he may have had a specific reason not to be seen with the other person. Don't forget, the ticket stubs we found show that the other person didn't go into the theater with Field. But these are all pointless speculations -\"\n[1377] Ellery smiled thoughtfully but said nothing. He thought to himself that the old man hadn't fully answered his question, and for a detective like Quin, who was more direct in his thinking, this was unusual...\n[1378] But Quin continued speaking. \"Well, we must always remember that the person who Field was dealing with may not be the killer. Of course, this is just one possibility. This crime seems to have been well-planned. But if that person isn't the killer, we need to find two people in the audience on Monday night who have a direct connection to Field's death.\" [1379] \"Morgan?\" Ellery asked casually. [1380] The detective shrugged. \"Perhaps. Why didn't he tell us that when we spoke to him yesterday afternoon? He confessed to everything else. Well, maybe he felt that paying a blackmail fee to a murdered man and being at the theater was enough indirect evidence.\" [1381] \"Let's look at this,\" Ellery said. \"We found a number '5,000' written on the flyer by the deceased, which obviously refers to money. Through Sampson and Cronin, we know that Field was a despicable man and possibly a criminal. Moreover, we learned from Morgan that he was good at extortion. Therefore, I think we can safely assume that he went to the Roman Theater on Monday night to collect or arrange the payment of the $50,000 ransom from someone we don't know yet. So far, it all makes sense, doesn't it?\" [1382] \"Continue,\" the inspector grumbled without expressing his opinion. [1383] \"Okay,\" Ellery continued, \"if we assume that the person who was being extorted and the murderer are the same person, we don't need to look for a motive anymore. The motive is clear - to stop Field from continuing to extort. However, if we assume that the murderer and the person being extorted are two different people, we have to continue looking for his motive. My view is that there is no need to do so - the murderer and the person being extorted are the same. What do you think?\" [1384] \"I tend to agree with you, Ellery,\" the inspector said. \"I only mentioned another possibility - without reaching a conclusion. So, let's continue on the assumption that the murderer and the person being extorted are the same...\"\n[1385] \"Now - I want to find out about the missing tickets.\" [1386] \"Ah \u2013 missing tickets,\" Ellery murmured. \"I wonder what you can make of that?\" [1387] \"Be serious now, you little rascal!\" Quin shouted. \"That's all I know. Overall, we have to deal with eight seats \u2013 one of which belongs to Field, and we found the stub on him; one belongs to the killer, and Flint found the stub for us; and finally, there are six empty seats, with tickets sold according to the ticket office records, but nowhere in the theater or the ticket office were the stubs found, either torn or intact \u2013 nothing at all. First, it's highly unlikely that all six tickets were in the theater on Monday evening, and then someone took them out. Remember, the search of everyone that night wasn't very thorough, and it's possible that we overlooked something small like a ticket. However, that's basically impossible. The best explanation is that either Field or the killer bought eight tickets, intending to use two and leave the other six, ensuring that they could not be disturbed during their brief transaction. In this case, the wisest course of action would be to destroy the tickets as soon as they were purchased. It could have been done by either Field or the killer, depending on who arranged the meeting. So, we must forget about those six tickets \u2013 they no longer exist, and we'll never find them. [1388] \"Next,\" the detective continued, \"we learned that Field and the person he was extorting entered the theater separately. This can be inferred from the fact that when I put the two stubs together, the tear didn't line up. If two people entered the theater together, the tickets would be handed to the usher together, and they would be torn together. This doesn't mean they didn't enter the theater at the same time; they may have come in one after the other for safety, pretending not to know each other. However, Maggie O'Connell claimed that no one sat in LL30 during the first act, and Jesse Lynch, the boy selling oranges, confirmed that no one sat there for at least ten minutes after the second act began. That means either the killer hadn't entered the theater yet or he had come in but sat somewhere else \u2013 he bought another ticket. \" [1389] Ellery shook his head. [1390] \"I realized that too, son,\" the old man said, a little irritated. \"I was just working through the thought process. What I meant to say was that it seems highly unlikely that the killer entered the theater at the time of the performance; he probably came in after the second act started and at least ten minutes later.\" [1391] \"I can prove that,\" Ellery said lazily. [1392] The officer took a pinch of snuff. \"I know - the mysterious numbers on the flyers. What do you make of them?\" [1393] 930\n[1394] 815\n[1395] 50,000\n[1396] \"We know what 50,000 represents. The other two numbers, however, do not refer to money but to time. First, '815' \u2013 the play started at 8:25. It is likely that Field arrived around 8:15 or even earlier, as he glanced at his watch for some reason. Now, let's assume that he had an appointment with someone, but that person was late. It is very likely that Field jotted down the numbers on the flyer casually \u2013 first, '50,000', indicating the transaction he was considering, including the $50,000 ransom; then 8:15, the time he was considering the matter; and finally 9:30 \u2013 the time the victim should have arrived! Writing these things down was natural for Field, as anyone with a habit of scribbling in their spare time would do so. We are lucky, as this indicates two things: first, the exact time Field met with the killer was 9:30; second, it corresponds to the time we hypothesize the murder took place. At 9:25, Lynch saw Field alive and alone; by 9:30, according to what Field wrote, the killer should have arrived, and we naturally assume the killer did arrive at the theater; and according to Dr. Jones, the poison that killed Field took 15 to 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 considering that Paskack found the body at 9:55, we can infer that the poison was taken at 9:35. If the tetrachloroethylene took 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 that would be 9:55. Of course, the killer had already left the crime scene by then. Don't forget, he couldn't have known that Mr. Paskack would suddenly stand up and leave his seat. The killer may have thought that Field's body would not be discovered until intermission, at 10:05, so Field would have been dead and unable to leave any further information. The killer was undoubtedly lucky, as he discovered Field too late and did not leave any other information, only stating that he was murdered. If Paskack had left five minutes earlier, we would have caught the cunning killer by now.\" [1397] \"Great!\" Ellery murmured, his smile full of affection, \"That was well stated, congratulations!\" [1398] \"Oh, go to hell,\" his father barked, \"I was just repeating what you said Monday night in Penczel's office \u2013 in fact, although the killer left the crime scene between 9:30 and 9:55, he was still in the theater for the rest of the evening, until we got everyone to leave.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBy the way, the morgue's car should be outside \u2013 I called for one on the way here. Goodbye.\" Plautti yawned and left in a lackluster manner. [368] As soon as Dr. Plautti left, two attendants in white clothes rushed through the carpet with a stretcher. With Officer Quinn's signal, they lifted the motionless body and placed it on the stretcher, covered it with a blanket, and hurriedly carried it away. As the terrible burden was carried away, the agents and police standing by the door breathed a sigh of relief \u2013 the main work of the evening was basically done. The audience rustled, shifted, coughed, and murmured. The sudden departure of the body reignited their interest. [369] Officer Quinn turned to Ellery, sighing wearily. At that moment, there was an unsettling commotion at the back of the theater. The audience stood up from their seats and looked in the direction of the commotion, and the police officers shouted for them to be quiet. Officer Quinn quickly spoke a few words to a policeman standing beside him. Ellery moved aside, his eyes shining. The commotion grew louder, and soon two policemen appeared, dragging a struggling man. They dragged the man to the front of the left aisle, grabbed his arms tightly, and pushed him to his feet. [370] The man was short and had a rat-like appearance. He was dressed in cheap, drab clothing and a shabby black hat that a country clergyman might wear occasionally. His mouth twisted into a grotesque shape and occasionally spewed out vicious curses. However, as soon as he noticed Officer Quinn staring at him, he stopped struggling and hung his head. [371] \"We found this man trying to sneak out through the side door of the theater on the other side, sir,\" one of the policemen panted, not forgetting to push the man roughly. [372] Inspector Quin smiled and took a snuff box out of his pocket, took a deep breath, and habitually sneezed. He smiled at the man who was silent and shrinking between the two policemen. [373] \"Oh, oh, Parsons,\" he said kindly, \"you came just in time, and it's so good.\" [374] Chapter 4 Two Suspects\n[375] Some people are naturally unable to tolerate grumbling men. The crowd was silent under the control of the police, but the man called \"the priest\" was pathetically shouting. Ellery felt disgusted with this man's ridiculous behavior. [376] Erlie watched him with disgust as he noticed the man's ridiculous behavior. Detective Quin's words were clearly sarcastic, and the \"Minister\" stiffened and glared at the officer before trying to break free from the strong arms holding him. He twisted, spit, and cursed, but eventually fell silent. He stopped cursing, but his body was still writhing in anger, and the men holding him were twisting and turning as well. Another policeman joined the fray, pinning the man to the ground. Suddenly, 