ged again, feigning a sincere, indignant innocent. Tears blurred his small eyes, like a true Christian martyr, while Inspector Quinn and his men were like pagan persecutors. The Reverend often used this expression to prove his innocence, and it usually worked. [388] \"Officer,\" he said, \"you know, you have no right to question me like this, don't you, officer? Everyone has the right to a lawyer, right? Definitely has the right!\" [389] Inspector Quinn stared curiously at him. \"When was the last time you saw Field?\" he asked. [390] \"Field? You mean Monte Field? I've never heard of him, sir. What are you trying to pin on me?\" the Reverend whispered anxiously. \"What's the charge?\" [391] \"Nothing, Parsons, no charges will be filed. But if you don't want to answer now, I can let you cool off. Maybe you'll say something later... But Parsons, don't forget, there are still some small things to investigate in the Bonomo Silk robbery case.\" [392] Ellery was startled when he heard his father speak as \"the priest\" was being dragged towards the back of the theater. \"This 'priest' is really stupid, isn't he?\" said the officer. \"Trying to slip away like that...\"\n[393] \"Thanks to these little tricks,\" Ellery smiled, \"one mistake can lead to twenty more.\" [394] Officer Quinn smiled and turned around as Wheeler approached with a stack of papers. [395] \"Ah, Thomas is back,\" said Officer Quinn, looking cheerful, \"What did you find, Thomas?\" [396] \"Officer, uh,\" Willie quickly flipped through the stack of records, \"It's hard to say. This is half of the list, the other half hasn't been recorded yet. But I think you might find something interesting.\" [397] He handed Inspector Quinn a stack of papers hastily recording names and addresses. These were the names that Willie had recorded while questioning the audience at the officer's request. [398] The inspector and Ellery, who was standing beside him, began to carefully examine the list, carefully studying each name. Just as they were halfway through, Inspector Quinn suddenly tensed up. He squinted at the name that made him stop, then looked at Willie with some confusion. [399] \"Morgan?\" he said thoughtfully, \"Benjamin Morgan. It sounds familiar, Willie. Have you thought of anything?\" [400] Willie smiled faintly. \"I knew you'd ask me that, officer. Benjamin Morgan was the lawyer partner of Monte Field, and they split up two years ago.\" [401] Quin nodded. The three exchanged glances. Then, Quin shrugged and said succinctly, \"I think we should learn more about Mr. Morgan.\" [402] He sighed and continued to look at the list, studying each name intently. He occasionally looked up, lost in thought, shook his head, and then continued reading. Wiley knew that Officer Quin had an excellent memory, even better than Ellery, so he watched his superior with admiration. [403] Finally, Officer Quin handed the list back to Wiley. \"Nothing there, Thomas,\" he said, his tone serious. \"Did you notice anything I missed?\" [404] Wiley stared at the older man for a moment, said nothing, and simply shook his head before walking away. [405] \"Wait a minute, Thomas,\" Officer Quin called out. \"Before you finish recording the second half, have Mr. Morgan come to the manager's office, please. But don't scare him. By the way, make sure he has a ticket stub before he goes to the office.\" Quin spoke, and Wiley left. [406] As the police were busy completing the tasks assigned by the detectives, Poulzer stood by watching. So, Quinn gestured for him to come over. The short, plump manager hurried over. [407] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn asked, \"when do your janitors usually start cleaning?\" [408] \"Oh, they've been here for a while, waiting to start, Officer. Most theaters clean up in the morning, but I always have them come in after the evening performance. What's your plan?\" [409] Ellery frowned slightly at Quinn's question but brightened up when he heard Poulzer's response, starting to polish his glasses contentedly. [410] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn continued calmly, \"what I want you to do is, tonight, after everyone leaves, you arrange for the female janitors to thoroughly clean the place. They must pick up and put away everything \u2013 everything, no matter how insignificant it may seem. Tell them to pay special attention to ticket stubs. Are they reliable?\" [411] \"Oh, absolutely reliable, officer. They've been here since the theater was built. You can trust they won't miss any clues. What should I do with the items they clean out?\" [412] \"Carefully package them, write my name on them, and have a trustworthy person deliver them to headquarters tomorrow morning.\" Detective Quin paused, then added, \"Mr. Pell, I want to emphasize: this task is extremely important, far more so than it appears. Do you understand?\" [413] \"Of course, of course!\" Pell hurriedly left after that. [414] A balding detective strode briskly across the carpet, descending the left aisle, and tipped his hat to Detective Quin. He held a stack of records, similar to those brought by Detective Wiley. [415] \"Detective Wiley asked me to give these to you. They're the remaining audience list and addresses, officer.\" [416] Detective Quinn eagerly took the document from the agent. Ellery leaned forward. The old man slowly scanned the paper with his long fingers, moving from one name to another. As he neared the last page, he smiled, glancing at Ellery with excitement before finishing the page. He turned to his son and whispered a few words. Ellery listened intently, nodding in agreement. [417] Officer Quinn turned back to the waiting detective and said, \"Johnson, come here,\" as he unfolded the paper he had been studying and showed it to Johnson, \"You go find Wells and have him report to me immediately. After that, you go find this woman\u2014\" his finger pointed to a name, along with the corresponding row and seat number, \"and have her come with you to the manager's office. You'll find that a man named Morgan is already there. By the way, if they start talking, listen in\u2014I need to know what they're saying. Treat the lady with respect.\" [418] \"Understood, sir. Johnson also told me,\" Johnson continued, \"that he separated those without tickets and other spectators. He wants to know how to handle them.\" [419] \"Have their names appeared on both records, Johnson?\" Quin asked, handing him the second record to take back to Waley. [420] \"All of them, sir.\" [421] \"Then tell Wheeler to let them leave with the others, but before they go, he should record their names separately. I won't need to see them or talk to them again.\" [422] Johnson saluted and left. [423] Detective Quinn turned to Ellery and spoke quietly. Ellery had some ideas in his mind, but Pendergast's reappearance interrupted them. [424] \"Detective?\" Manager Pendergast coughed politely. [425] \"Oh, what is it, Pendergast?\" Detective Quinn quickly turned around and said, \"Is the cleaning lady doing well?\" [426] \"Yes, sir. Do you have any other orders? And, officer, I beg your pardon, I'd like to ask, how long will the audience have to wait? Many people have asked me this question, and I hope there won't be any trouble on this matter.\" [427] \"Oh, don't worry, Pensel,\" the officer said casually, \"they'll be leaving soon. In fact, I've already ordered my men to let them go in a few minutes. However, before they leave, there's something that will make them complain.\" [428] \"Really, sir?\" [429] \"Yes,\" Quinn said, \"they will be searched. They will undoubtedly protest, and you will hear their threats to sue you or even hit you. But don't worry. I'll take responsibility for everything that happened tonight, and I promise you won't have any trouble... Now, I need a female searcher to help us. We have a female officer here, but she's busy downstairs. You see, can you find a reliable woman for me? - preferably a middle-aged woman - who can accept this ungrateful job and keep it secret.\" [430] The manager thought for a moment. \"I think I can help you. Mrs. Philip, she is our costume custodian. She has been working here for many years and will definitely meet your expectations.\" [431] \"That's her,\" Quin said cheerfully, \"find her and send her to the main entrance. Inspector Willie will give her the necessary instructions.\" [432] Willie, who was walking over, heard the last sentence. Pensel hurried down the aisle, heading for the box. [433] \"Is Morgan ready?\" Quin asked. [434] \"Yes, sir.\" [435] \"Good, then you have one more thing to do, Thomas. I want you to supervise the audience in the main hall and the boxes, making sure they leave one by one. Thoroughly search them before they leave. Everyone must leave through the main entrance, and make sure the guards at other exits direct the audience to the back of the theater.\" [436] Williams nodded. \"Now, regarding the search - Piggott!\" [437] Erlie, who had been leaning lazily against a pillar, stood up and followed Piggott. Williams hurried over, and Quinn called out, \"Don't let the audience upstairs leave yet. Wait until the front row audience leaves before they can go. Send someone up to maintain order and keep them quiet.\" [438] After giving the last important instructions, Quinn turned to the guard nearby, Dole, and whispered, \"Go to the cloakroom, Dole. Keep a close eye on the coat when someone takes it. When they leave, thoroughly search the place. Bring anything left behind to me.\" [439] Erlie leaned against a pillar again, like a marble sentinel standing at the crime scene. He stood there, his eyes glazed over, his hands tightly gripping the collar of his coat. Flint, a broad-shouldered man, rushed over, his eyes shining with excitement. Detective Quinn looked at him cautiously. [440] \"Any discoveries, Flint?\" he asked, fumbling for his snuff box. [441] \"Very good, very good!\" Detective Quinn called out, \"Where did you find it?\" [442] \"Right inside the main entrance,\" Flint replied. \"It looks like the ticket holder dropped it as soon as they came in.\" [443] Detective Quinn didn't speak. His hand suddenly reached into his vest pocket and pulled out the blue ticket stub found on the deceased. He silently looked at them \u2013 two ticket stubs, the same color, one with \"LL32 Left\" printed on it, and the other with \"LL30 Left.\" [444] As he closely examined the innocent ticket stubs, his eyes narrowed into a line. He bent down to take a closer look, carefully aligning the bottoms of the ticket stubs; a hint of confusion appeared in his gray eyes, and then he aligned the tops of the ticket stubs. But no matter how he tried, the two ticket stubs did not match at the tear. [445] Nevertheless, the tearing positions of the two tickets were not consistent with each other. [446] Chapter 5: Interrogating Suspects\n[447] Officer Quinn walked across the wide red carpet laid in front of the theater's main entrance, lowering his hat to cover his eyes. He reached into his pocket, feeling for his indispensable snuff box. Officer Quinn seemed to be deep in thought, as he tightly held the two blue stubs, furrowing his brow in dissatisfaction. [448] He approached a door with green spots, marked \"Manager's Office.\" Before opening it, he turned around to survey the situation. The commotion among the audience had subsided significantly. Chatter filled the theater as police and investigators moved between the rows, issuing commands, answering questions, and ushering the audience out of their seats, lining them up in the main aisle to be searched at the main entrance. [449] Officer Quinn inadvertently noticed that very few people protested the tormenting ordeal. They seemed too exhausted to complain about the rude search. [450] The office's reception area was small, decorated with copper and oak. Parsons Johnny sat slumped in a chair against the wall, puffing away indifferently. A police officer stood next to the chair, a large hand 