eferred to this office as the \"Star Chamber\". Although small, the office was comfortable, like a home. Ellery lazily sat in the chair and began to read the books about handwriting he had stolen from Field's apartment and office. The detective pressed the buzzer, and soon Thomas Wiley's sturdy figure appeared in his sight. [1030] \"Good morning, Thomas,\" Quinn said, \"What important news did you bring from Brown Brothers Company?\" [1031] \"I don't know how important this news is, Inspector,\" Wiley said calmly, sitting down on a straight-backed chair along the wall, \"But it sounds useful to me. Last night, you asked me to investigate Field's hat. Well, I have an exact replica here on my desk. Would you like to see it?\" [1032] \"Don't be silly, Thomas,\" Queer said, \"Go get it!\" Wiley got up and came back with a hat box in a short time. He untied the ribbon and took out a magnificent hat of excellent quality. Queer couldn't help but stare curiously at the hat. The size inside was marked: seven and eight-eighths. [1033] \"I talked to an employee at Brown's, he's an old pro,\" Wiley continued, \"He's been serving Field for years. It seems that all the threads and needles on Field's body were bought there - for a long time. Coincidentally, he also likes to find the same employee. Naturally, the old employee knows all about Field's taste and purchase records. [1034] \"He said, first, Field is very particular about his clothes. His clothes are custom-made at Brown's Special Tailoring Shop. He likes expensive clothes and cuts, and favors the latest undergarments and collars... ...\"\n[1035] \"And what about the hat?\" Ellery asked without looking up. [1036] \"I was just getting to that, sir,\" Wiley continued. \"The employee emphasized the hat issue. For example, when I asked about the hat, he said, 'Mr. Field is obsessed with hats. In the past six months, he has bought at least three hats!' Of course, I took note of that\u2014he checked the sales records. It was undeniable that Field bought three silk hats in the past six months!\" [1037] Ellery and his father exchanged glances, both thinking of the same question. [1038] \"Three\u2014\" the old man began to ask. [1039] \"Now... isn't that an unusual situation?\" Ellery asked slowly, reaching for his spectacles. [1040] \"Where are the other two hats?\" Quin asked, with a puzzled expression on his face. [1041] Ellery remained silent. [1042] Quine turned impatiently to Wiley. \"Do you have any other discoveries, Thomas?\" [1043] \"Nothing of value, except for one thing,\" Wiley answered, \"which is that Mr. Field has become addicted to buying clothes, even buying fifteen suits and at least six hats, including fedoras, in the past year alone!\" [1044] \"Hats, hats, hats!\" the detective muttered, \"This man must be insane. Listen, have you found out if Mr. Field bought a walking stick at Brown's company?\" [1045] Wiley's face showed a look of panic. \"Ah... er, Detective, I'm afraid I overlooked that point. I never thought to ask that question, and you didn't tell me to -\"\n[1046] \"Damn it! We always miss something,\" Quin roared. \"Call that employee, Thomas.\" [1047] Weaver picked up a phone on the table. After a while, he handed the phone to his superior. [1048] \"I'm Inspector Quin,\" the old man said quickly. \"I know you've been serving Field for a long time... yes, I just want to check some details. Has Field bought a cane from you?... What? Oh, I see... Yes. And one more thing: did he make any special requests when making clothes, like adding extra pockets, for example?... You don't think so? Okay... What? Oh, I see. Thank you very much.\" [1049] He hung up the phone and turned around. [1050] \"Our late friend,\" he said disdainfully, \"seems to have the same aversion to canes as he does to hats. The employee said he repeatedly tried to get Field interested in canes, but Field rejected them every time. He said he didn't like canes. And the employee confirmed that Field did not have any special pockets sewn\u2014no, he didn't do it. So, it looks like we're in a dead end again.\" [1051] \"On the contrary,\" Ellery said calmly, \"this does not lead us into a dead end, but rather confirms that the only thing the killer took last night was the hat. In my opinion, this makes the problem simpler.\" [1052] \"I'm still too stupid,\" his father muttered, \"I don't see any meaning in this.\" [1053] \"By the way, Inspector,\" Wiley said with a furrowed brow, \"Jimmy reported the results of the fingerprint investigation on the bottle. There were some fingerprints, he said, but they were all Mr. Field's. Of course, he had obtained Mr. Field's fingerprints from the morgue and checked them. \" [1054] \"Well,\" the officer said, \"maybe the bottle has nothing to do with the case. Anyway, we have to wait for Prouty's lab report.\" [1055] \"One more thing, Inspector,\" Wiley added, \"the garbage - the garbage from the Roman Theater that you asked Pensel to send over this morning - has just arrived. Do you want to take a look?\" [1056] \"Of course, Thomas,\" Quine said, \"when you go out, bring me the list you made last night, the one that records all the people without tickets. Each name should have a seat number, right?\" [1057] Welles nodded and left. Quine looked worriedly at his son's head as Welles returned with a bulky package and a printed list. [1058] They carefully spread out the contents of the package on the desk. Most of them were crumpled promotional flyers, torn pieces of paper from candy boxes, and many ticket stubs - all of which Flint and his crew had missed. There were also various styles of women's gloves, a brown button that might have fallen off a man's overcoat, a pen cap, a handkerchief for a woman, and other miscellaneous items that are often lost or thrown away in a theater. [1059] \"It seems there's nothing of value, right?\" the officer commented, \"Well, at least we can check the ticket stubs.\" [1060] Welles piled up the