nt balance, he wouldn't use whole numbers.\" [1092] \"We can easily verify or refute this,\" the officer countered, picking up the phone. He had the police station operator connect him to Field's office. After talking to Oscar L\u00fcwin for a while, he turned dejectedly towards Ellery. [1093] \"You're right, Ellery,\" he said. \"Field's personal savings are very little. Although he often deposits 10,000 or 15,000 dollars, all his deposits amount to less than 6,000 dollars, and L\u00fcwin is surprised by this. He only found out about Field's personal financial situation when I asked him. I bet Field probably gambles or bets on horses.\" [1094] \"That's in line with my expectations,\" Ellery said, \"which means there's a possible explanation for the '50,000' on the program sheet. The number doesn't just represent fifty thousand dollars, but it also indicates a deal, a transaction amounting to fifty thousand dollars! To make so much in one night is quite impressive, but unfortunately, he didn't live to see the money.\" [1095] \"What about the other two numbers?\" Quin asked. [1096] \"Whatever the transaction is,\" the officer said succinctly, \"you can be sure it's a sinful one.\" [1097] \"Sinful transaction?\" Ellery asked skeptically. [1098] \"Money is the root of all evil,\" the officer argued with a smile. [1099] Ellery's tone remained unchanged as he continued, \"Not just the source, Father - but the result as well.\" [1100] Ellery's tone remained unchanged as he continued, \"Not just the source, Father - but the result as well.\" [1101] \"Another quote?\" the old man scoffed. [1102] \"Quoted from Fielding,\" Ellery replied calmly. [1103] Chapter 11: Shadows of the Past\n[1104] The phone rang. [1105] \"Quinn? It's Sampson,\" the voice on the other end of the line said, belonging to the district attorney. [1106] \"Good morning, Henry,\" Quinn said. \"Where are you? How are you feeling this morning?\" [1107] \"I'm in the office and feeling terrible,\" Sampson replied with a chuckle. \"The doctor insists that if I keep going like this, I'll be dead soon; the government insists that if I don't come to work, the city will be ruined. What am I supposed to do? ... Quinn?\" [1108] Quinn winked at Ellery across the table, as if to say, \"I know what's coming next.\" [1109] \"Well, what is it, Henry?\" [1110] \"There's a gentleman in my private office now, and I think you should meet him. It would be to your advantage,\" Sampson continued softly. \"He wants to see you, and I think you should stop what you're doing and come over right away. He's\u2014\" Sampson lowered his voice, \"He's someone I can't afford to offend, Quinn, old friend.\" [1111] Quinn furrowed his brow. \"I guess you mean Mr. Eves-Pope,\" he said. \"He's upset because we asked about his daughter last night?\" [1112] \"Not exactly,\" said Sampson. \"He's a decent man. It's just\u2014uh\u2014just be nice to him, Quinn, can you?\" [1113] \"I'll be very gentle with him,\" the old man said with a smile. \"To put your mind at ease, I'll bring my son along. He's usually very concerned about our social responsibilities.\" [1114] \"That's good,\" said Sampson gratefully. [1115] The police officer hung up the phone and turned to Ellery. \"Poor Henry's in trouble,\" he teased. \"It's no wonder, he's always trying to please others. He's sick as a dog, politicians are giving him a hard time, and now this millionaire is yelling at him in his office... Come on, son, let's meet the infamous Franklin Eves-Pope!\" [1116] Ellery stretched his arms and groaned, \"If this keeps up, you'll have another patient here. Anyway, I'm getting up, putting my hat on tight, and let's go see this industry titan.\" [1117] Quinn grinned at Willie. \"Before I forget, Thomas... I want you to do some more investigating today. You mainly check why the lawyer for Field's office is living like a king, but his personal savings are only $6,000. He might have invested in Wall Street or horse racing, and I wan