ts, and I looked at her closely. She was pale with fear, but still bold enough to start cursing at me, asking who I was, what I was doing in her apartment at night, and so on. I showed her my badge. Detective, the strong, beautiful woman immediately clammed up when she saw my badge, refusing to answer my questions.\" [818] \"Why is that?\" the old man's gaze shifted from the floor to the ceiling, surveying the room's furnishings. [819] \"It's hard to say, detective,\" Ritter said. \"At first, she seemed scared, but after seeing the badge, she became more confident. The longer I stayed here, the more brazen she became.\" [820] \"You didn't tell her about Field, did you?\" Detective Quinn asked in a low, urgent tone. [821] Ritter glared at his superior. \"Not a word, sir,\" he said. \"I couldn't get anything out of her \u2013 she kept yelling, 'Wait for Monte to come back, you jerk!' \u2013 so I checked the bedroom, which was empty, and then I pushed her into the bedroom, turned on the lights, didn't close the door, and stayed here all night. She eventually climbed into bed, and I think she fell asleep. At around seven this morning, she suddenly came out and started yelling at me again, as if she thought Field had been arrested by headquarters and demanded a newspaper. I told her to calm down and then called the office. That's all that happened since then.\" [822] \"Say, Dad!\" Ellery suddenly called from a corner of the room. \"What do you think our lawyer friend was reading? You'll never guess \u2013 it was 'How to Judge Character from Handwriting!'\" [823] Detective Quin stood up and grumbled, \"Stop messing around with those books and come here.\" [824] He opened the bedroom door, and the woman was sitting on the bed with her legs crossed. The bed was of poor quality and was in the French style, with a thick silk canopy that hung from the ceiling to the floor. Haggstrom was now staring blankly out the window. [825] Detective Quin quickly looked around. He turned to Ritter and whispered, \"Was the bed this messy when you came in last night? \u2013 did it look like someone had slept on it?\" [826] Quinn turned to the woman and walked over to the bedside, sitting next to her and looking at her half-turned face. She lit a cigarette with a disdainful look. [827] \"I'm Inspector Quinn, dear,\" the old man said gently. \"I remind you that if you persist in not answering or lying, you will encounter more trouble. I'm sure you understand that.\" [828] She twisted away. \"I won't answer any of your questions, Inspector, until I know what power you have over me,\" she said. \"I haven't done anything illegal, and I've been behaving properly. Think about it.\" [829] Quinn took a pinch of snuff, as if the woman's words reminded him of his favorite thing. He spoke in a pleasant voice, \"That's fair, you see, all alone, rushing out of bed in the middle of the night - were you in bed then?\" [830] Quinn took a pinch of snuff, as if the woman's words reminded him of his favorite thing. He spoke in a pleasant voice, \"That's fair, you see, all alone, rushing out of bed in the middle of the night - were you in bed then?\" [831] \"Of course I was in bed,\" she said immediately, biting her lips tightly. [832] \"\u2014facing a police officer... I don't think you were scared, my dear.\" [833] \"No!\" she exclaimed. [834] \"Well, we won't argue about that,\" the old man said kindly, \"but you don't mind telling me your name, do you?\" [835] \"I don't know why I should tell you, but it won't do me any harm.\" The woman argued, \"My name is Angela Lasso\u2014Mrs. Angela Lasso\u2014well, I'm engaged to Field.\" [836] \"I see, Mrs. Angela Roxo,\" Quine said gravely. \"You're engaged to Field. Well, what were you doing in the room last night, Mrs. Angela Roxo?\" [837] \"It's none of your business!\" she said coldly. \"You'd better let me go now\u2014I haven't done anything illegal. You have no right to ask me these ridiculous questions, old man!\" [838] 