 not involved at all? We must remember, according to Morgan, that Cazzanelli and Field had a close relationship. Field was his lawyer, and perhaps he bribed Parsons to help with the irregular transactions Croaning has been investigating. If Parsons being at the theater wasn't a coincidence, was it through Field or O'Connell? Is it as they both claimed? I think, my son,\" he tugged at his beard, adding, \"I want to give Parsons Johnny a taste of the whip\u2014it won't hurt his thick skin! And that little proud O'Connell girl\u2014scaring her won't do much harm either...\"\n[1402] He took a deep breath of snuff, followed by a series of sneezes, and Ellery's sympathetic laughter accompanied him. [1403] \"And dear old Benjamin Morgan,\" the officer continued, \"the anonymous letter conveniently provided him with a ticket, the source of which was very mysterious. Is he lying about this?\" [1404] \"And then there's the most interesting lady, Mrs. Angela Roxboro... Ah! These ladies, bless them! They always muddle up a man's logic. What did she say\u2014that she'd be at Field's flat by nine-thirty? That her absence was evidence? Of course, the porter confirmed her story. But it's easy to fix a porter... For Field's business\u2014especially his private business\u2014does she know more than she's letting on? Was she lying when she said Field told her he'd be back at the flat at ten? Remember, we know the meeting at the Roman Theater was at nine-thirty\u2014did he really think he could be back home by ten? By taxi, it would take fifteen or twenty minutes on the road, leaving only ten minutes for the transaction\u2014perhaps, of course. The subway wouldn't be much faster. We must remember, this woman didn't show up at the theater all evening.\" [1405] \"Just this beautiful woman alone will keep you busy,\" Ellery said. \"It's obvious that she's hiding something. Have you noticed her brazen contempt? It's not just bluffing. She knows something, Dad. I'll be watching her closely - she'll slip up eventually.\" [1406] \"Haggstrom will take care of her.\" Quinn said absent-mindedly, \"Now, what about Michaels? He doesn't have an alibi for Monday night. But that doesn't matter. He wasn't at the theater... Did he really go to Field's apartment on Tuesday morning to look for something? We've thoroughly searched Field's apartment\u2014did we miss something? He lied about the check thing, and obviously didn't know Field was dead. He must have realized he was taking a risk coming to Field's apartment. He read the paper, he wouldn't want the police there. So, what's his reason for taking the risk?\" [1407] \"Maybe something related to his prison term\u2014I mentioned that, and he looked surprised, right?\" Ellery chuckled. [1408] \"Maybe.\" The detective replied, \"By the way, I found out from Weil about Michaels' stint in Elmira. Thomas reported it was a hush-hush case\u2014much heavier than the light sentence he got in the reformatory. Michaels had been suspected of forgery\u2014it looked serious for him. Then his lawyer, Field, cleverly defended him, and he was charged with a completely different offense\u2014something like a minor theft\u2014and that was the end of the forgery charges. This Michaels seems to have some pull\u2014we have to keep a close eye on him.\" [1409] \"I have a little theory about Michaels,\" Ellery said thoughtfully, \"but let's put that aside for now.\" [1410] Quinn seemed not to have heard Ellery. He stared into the crackling fire in the hearth. \"And Lune,\" he said, \"a man like Lune, so closely connected to his employer, claiming he knows nothing else besides what his employer mentions, seems too hard to believe. Is he hiding something? If so, he'd better pray\u2014because Croaning will soon make him reveal the truth.\" [1411] \"I like Croning,\" Ellery said, \"How does a man muster the determination to do something like that... Have you thought of a question? I want to know if Morgan knew Angela Roxo. Despite both denying it, wouldn't it be interesting if they did?\" [1412] \"Son,\" Quinn complained, \"stop worrying yourself. We have enough on our plate already, so... Ah!\" [1413] The room was quiet and comfortable. Quinn lay down, the flickering firelight illuminating him. Ellery munched on a pastry. Juno sat in a corner, eyes shining with excitement, her small body hunched as she listened to their conversation. [1414] Suddenly, in a moment of mental shift, the old man's gaze met Ellery's. [1415] \"Hats...,\" Quinn murmured, \"We always come back to the hat.\" [1416] Ellery's gaze was puzzled. \"Back to the hat is fine, Dad. Hat - hat - hat! What does the hat have to do with the case? How much do we know about the hat?\" [1417] The officer shifted in his chair. He propped his feet up, took a pinch of snuff, and continued the discussion with renewed vigor. \"Well. We can't be lazy about that damned silk hat. So far, what do we know? First, the hat is still in the theater. Isn't that interesting? After such a thorough search, we couldn't find any trace, which seems impossible... Everyone left, and there was nothing in the cloakroom; there was nothing found in the debris, nothing to suggest that the hat was torn or burned; in fact, there was no clue, no evidence to help us continue the investigation. Therefore, Ellery, in this regard, the only reasonable judgment is that we are looking in the wrong place! Further, wherever it is, it must still be in the theater, because we have been closed since Monday evening. Ellery, we will go back to the theater tomorrow and turn it upside down. Unless we see some light, I won't sleep.\" [1418] Ellery was silent for a moment. \"I'm not satisfied with what you said, Dad,\" he said finally, \"Hat - hat - something is wrong!\" [1419] The old man nodded vigorously. \"Since yesterday morning, when I had time to think about the hat, I felt that we were stuck somewhere. It's already Wednesday evening, and there's no progress. We've done all the necessary work, but we've got nothing... I stared into the fire in the fireplace, \"Everything is a mess. I know all the ins and outs, but for some reason, I can't seem to put them together - explain everything... Son, the missing piece is the key to the whole case.\" [1420] The phone rang. The officer jumped up to answer the phone. He listened carefully to a man's slow speech, commented lightly, and then hung up the phone. [1421] \"It's deep in the middle of the night, who is it? And you talk so much. Oh, you know a lot of secrets, right?\" Ellery grinned. [1422] \"It's Edmund Krueger.\" Quin said, \"You remember, I told him to go check the Roman Theater yesterday morning. He has been there for two days, yesterday and today. And he clearly reported that there was no secret hiding place in the theater. Edmund Krueger has the final say on such architectural issues, and if he says there's no hiding place, you can be sure there won't be any.\" [1423] He stood up and saw Junna squatting in the corner. \"Junna! Make the bed.\" He yelled. Junna quietly walked through the room and left with a silent smile. Quin turned to Ellery, who had taken off his coat and was fumbling with his tie. [1424] \"The first thing we have to do tomorrow morning is go to the Roman Theater and search again.\" The old man spoke firmly, \"Tell you, son \u2013 I hate wasting time! Some people had better watch out!\" [1425] Ellery wrapped his father's shoulders with his muscular arm affectionately. \"Go to sleep, you old cheat!\" He laughed. [1426] This book is compiled by \"ePUBw.COM\". \"ePUBw.COM\" provides the latest and most complete e-book downloads! [1427] Part Three\n[1428] \"Great detectives are born, not made. Like all geniuses, they do not come from the meticulously cultivated police, but from the whole of humanity. The most amazing detective I have ever known is an old, dirty shaman who has never left the jungle... This truly outstanding detective has a unique gift \u2014 extraordinary observation of events, understanding of human thinking, and insight into the human mind. And this gift is the factor that solves the problem, or the unshakable law.\" [1429] \u2014 Excerpt from \"The Hunter's Guide\" by James Redix, Jr.\n[1430] Chapter 14: The Hat Problem Grows Larger\n[1431] On Thursday, September 27, the day after the murder at the Roman Theater, Inspector Queequeg and Ellery got up early and hastily dressed. Juno was dragged out of bed and dressed in plain clothes, looking like a maid in the Queequeg household. They gobbled down a hurried breakfast in Juno's disapproving gaze. [1432] They chewed their tasteless pancakes loudly, and the old man asked Juno to put through a call to Louis Pensel. After a while, the inspector spoke kindly into the phone: \"Good morning, Pensel. I apologize for waking you up so early... Something important has happened, and we need your help. Can you come to the Roman Theater to open it for us? I told you that the theater would not be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1433] Pensel mumbled a comforting phrase sleepily. [1434] \"Can you come to the Roman Theater right away to open it for us?\" the old man continued, \"I told you that the theater wouldn't be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. You know, I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1435] \"Great!\" Pensel's voice trembled with excitement over the phone line. \"You want me to come to the theater right away? I'll be there in half an hour \u2013 I'm not even dressed yet.\" [1436] \"Alright,\" Quin replied. \"Of course, Pencier \u2013 don't let anyone in yet. Wait for us on the sidewalk before you unlock the door, and don't tell anyone. We'll discuss it further inside the theater... Wait a moment.\" [1437] He held the phone to his chest and looked at Ellery with a questioning gaze. Ellery gestured vigorously, and Quin mouthed a name, nodding in agreement before returning to the phone. [1438] \"Now there's one more thing I need your help with, Pencier,\" he continued. \"Can you find Mrs. Philip, the enthusiastic lady? I'd like her to meet us at the theater as soon as possible.\" [1439] \"Certainly, Inspector,\" Pencier said. The inspector hung up the phone. [1440] \"Well, that's settled,\" he said, rubbing his hands together and reaching into his pocket for a snuff box. \"Ah \u2013 ah \u2013 sneeze! Thank you, Sir Walter and all the hardworking pioneers who defended the tobacco industry!\" He sneezed happily. \"We'll be there in a moment, Ellery.\" [1441] He picked up the phone again and issued a few cheerful orders to the detective headquarters before slamming the phone back onto the table and urging Ellery to put on his coat. Juno looked mournfully at them as they left. He often begged the officer to let him accompany them on their occasional trips to the secluded paths of New York, but the officer had his own ideas about raising teenagers and always refused. Juno regarded his benefactor as if he were a totemic object from the Stone Age, so he had to accept this inevitable outcome and hope for better luck in the future. [1442] It was a cold, damp day. Ellery and his father pulled up their coat collars and walked towards Broadway and the subway. Both were unusually quiet, but their faces expressed a sense of eager anticipation - an uncanny similarity, yet so different - that promised to be an exciting and meaningful day. [1443] They walked briskly along 47th Street towards the Roman Theater. The morning wind was biting, and the streets of Broadway and its spiderweb-like alleys were deserted. A man in a brown coat loitered in front of the closed glass doors of the lobby; another leaned comfortably against the high iron grille separating the left alley from the street. In front of the theater's main entrance, they could see the short, stout Louis Pincus standing and talking to Flint. [1444] Pincus shook hands enthusiastically. \"Well, the ban is finally lifted... it's so nice to hear the news, officer,\" he said. [1445] Pincus fished out a large key ring and unlocked the lobby door. The four of them filed in. The dark-skinned manager fumbled with the lock on the inner door and then pushed it open. The darkened main hall lay before them. [1446] Ellery shuddered. \"Perhaps only the Metropolitan Opera House and Tess's grave are more lifeless than this theatre. It's the perfect place for a deceased relative.\" [1447] The officer wasn't as verbose. He grumbled and pushed his son into the darkened auditorium. \"Cut it out! You're making us nervous.\" [1448] Pincus quickly stepped forward and turned on the main power switch. Under the glare of the giant arc lights and chandeliers, the auditorium took on its familiar contours. Ellery's fanciful metaphor was not as bizarre as his father had suggested. Filthy waterproof canvas covered the rows of long seats; shadows fell on the already dusty carpet; the white plaster wall behind the empty stage was bare, forming an ugly blotch amidst the red plush. [1449] \"I apologize for the waterproof canvas,\" the officer murmured to Pincus, \"because we have to roll them up. We're going to conduct a small-scale search of the auditorium front. Flint, please bring in the two men outside. They might as well make some extra money.\" [1450] Flint hurried out and returned with the two detectives waiting outside the theatre. Under the officer's instructions, they began to pull the large coverings off the rubber chairs, revealing rows of padded seats. Ellery stood near the left aisle, pulling out a small notebook from his pocket, which he had used to sketch the theatre layout on Monday night. He pondered silently, biting his lower lip. He occasionally looked up, as if to verify the layout of the theatre. [1451] Quin quickly returned to the back of the theater, where Poulson was pacing nervously. \"Poulson, we're going to be here for hours. I didn't bring enough people. Can you help me... I need to think about something... just take up a little of your time, but it'll be very helpful to me.\" [1452] \"Of course, Officer!\" the short manager replied. \"I'll be happy to help.\" [1453] The officer coughed. \"Don't think I'm treating you like a runner or anything, old pal,\" he explained apologetically. \"But I need someone who's trained in searching for things like this... and I need some important information from a few people in the district attorney's office who are investigating other aspects of the case. Can you help me deliver a note to a man named Cronin... and bring back a package he gives you? I'm sorry to ask you to do this, Poulson, but it's too important for me to trust it to just any messenger, and\u2014I'm in a bind.\" [1454] Poulson smiled brightly. \"Don't worry about it, Officer. If you need a note, I have paper and pen in my office.\" [1455] The two returned to Poulson's office. Five minutes later, they returned to the auditorium. Poulson hurried out onto the street, holding a sealed letter. Quin watched him leave, sighed, and turned to Ellery. Ellery sat on the armrest of the chair where Field had been killed, still contemplating the pencil sketches he had made. [1456] The officer whispered something to his son. Ellery smiled and patted the older man on the back. [1457] \"Son, shall we take action?\" Quick said. \"I forgot to ask Pencier if he notified Mrs. Philip. I guess he did, or he would have said so. Where is she anyway?\" [1458] Flint was busy removing the waterproof canvas cover with two other detectives, and Quick motioned for him to come over. [1459] \"I told you to do some stretching this morning, Flint. Go up and get busy.\" [1460] \"What am I looking for today, Officer?\" the burly detective grinned. \"Because I hope my luck is better than Monday night.\" [1461] \"You're looking for a hat - a fancy, shiny hat, the kind a stylish person wears, son,\" the officer said solemnly. \"But if you see anything else, call me out loud!\" [1462] Ellery reluctantly took off his coat and slipped the pamphlet into his pocket. The officer twisted and turned to remove his overcoat, leading his son into the corridor. They began to search the audience together, slowly and methodically.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[548] \"That's good,\" Quine approved. \"So, when was the last time you saw Monty Field?\" [549] Morgan cleared his throat uneasily. \"Er... I haven't seen him in a while,\" he whispered in response. \"You see, we used to be partners - successful in legal matters. Then some things happened, and we went our separate ways. Since then, I... I haven't seen him again.\" [550] \"How long ago was that?\" [551] \"About two years and a bit.\" [552] \"Very good.\" Quinn leaned forward. \"I also want to know why you split up.\" [553] Morgan looked down at the carpet, fiddling with his cigar. \"I... uh, I guess you know what kind of person Field is. We had a disagreement about ethics, and after a small argument, we decided to go our separate ways.\" [554] \"Did you part on good terms?\" [555] \"Uh... in that situation, yes.\" [556] Quinn tapped the table. Morgan shifted uncomfortably. He clearly hadn't fully recovered from the shock. [557] \"What time did you arrive at the theater tonight, Morgan?\" the officer asked. [558] Morgan seemed surprised by the question. \"Ah\u2014around 8:15,\" he replied. [559] \"Let me see your ticket stub,\" Quinn said. [560] Morgan fumbled through his pockets before producing it and handing it to Quinn. Quinn took the ticket stub, then pulled out three more from his pockets, placing them under the table and carefully comparing them. He then returned the four ticket stubs to his pocket without expression. [561] \"So, you were sitting in the middle M2 seat, right?\" he said. \"Nice spot, Morgan. What made you want to see 'Gunplay'?\" [562] \"Ah, it's a novelty show, isn't it, officer?\" Morgan looked a bit embarrassed. \"I didn't even know I'd want to come to the theater \u2013 you know, I don't usually go to the theater \u2013 but the manager of the Roman Theater sent me a free ticket for tonight's performance, so I came.\" [563] \"Is that so?\" Quine asked bluntly. \"When did you receive the ticket?\" [564] \"Ah, I got the letter on Saturday morning, officer, delivered to my office.\" [565] \"Oh, you got a letter too, did you? You don't happen to have it on you, do you?\" [566] \"I\u2014very\u2014sure\u2014I brought it,\" he mumbled, starting to rummage through his pockets, \"Here it is! Here you go.\" [567] He handed the officer a small rectangular piece of rough-edged paper made of wrinkled securities printing paper. Officer Quinn carefully took it to the light. Through the printed lines, a clear watermark pattern could be seen. He pursed his lips and carefully placed the paper on the desk's notepad. Under Morgan's gaze, he opened the drawer above Posen's desk and searched until he found a sheet of paper. The paper was large and square, with a luxurious theater seal printed on it. Quinn placed the two papers side by side, pondered for a moment, then sighed, picked up the one Morgan handed him, and read it slowly. [568] The Roman Theater Department cordially invites Benjamin Morgan, Esq. to attend the performance of 'Gunfight' on September 24th (Monday) evening. We sincerely hope that Mr. Morgan, a leading figure in the legal profession in New York, will share his valuable opinions on the social and legal aspects of the play. Of course, we do not impose any obligation. The Roman Theater Department guarantees that Mr. Morgan can accept this invitation without any obligation. [569] (Signature) Roman Theater\n[570] Inviter: S.\n[571] The \"S\" written in ink was sloppy and almost unrecognizable. [572] Quin looked up, smiled. \"Roman Theatre is quite something, Mr. Morgan. I was wondering -\" he continued to smile, gesturing towards Johnson, who had been sitting quietly in the corner, listening to their conversation. [573] \"Call the manager, Johnson,\" Quin said, \"if that publicity man - that Bellson, or Pearson, or whoever - is here, have him come too.\" [574] After Johnson left, Quin turned back to Morgan. [575] \"Could you show me your gloves, Mr. Morgan?\" he whispered. [576] Morgan looked at him perplexed and then threw the gloves on the table in front of him. Quinn picked them up curiously. They were a pair of white silk gloves \u2014 typically worn with evening attire. Quinn pretended to be busy examining the gloves. He turned them over, closely inspecting a smudge on one fingertip, and even tried to put them on his own hand, joking with Morgan. After his examination, he returned the gloves to Morgan with a serious expression. [577] \"Ah \u2014 oh, by the way, Mr. Morgan \u2014 your hat is quite a sight. May I see it?\" [578] Morgan placed the hat on the table, still silent. Quinn picked it up nonchalantly, whistling a tune called \"Broadway,\" a few notes lower than standard pitch. He flipped the hat in his hand. It was a high-quality hat, with a lustrous silk lining and a gold stamp of the manufacturer, \"James Chalmers & Co.\" The initials \"B.M.\" were embroidered on the band. [579] Quinn grinned and put the hat on his own head. It fit perfectly. But he quickly took it off and handed it back to Morgan. [580] \"Thank you very much for allowing me to examine your gloves and hat, Mr. Morgan,\" he said as he pulled out a notepad from his pocket and jotted down a note hastily. [581] The door opened, and Johnson, Pensel, and Harry Nelson entered. Pensel hesitated before approaching, while Nelson sat in an armchair. [582] \"What can I do for you, officer?\" Pensel asked nervously, trying to ignore the dejected, silver-haired figure sitting in the chair. [583] \"Mr. Pensel,\" Quinn said calmly, \"In your Roman Theatre, how many kinds of writing paper do you have?\" [584] The manager's eyes widened. \"Just one, officer. There's one right in front of you on the table.\" [585] \"Ah \u2013\" Quinn handed Pensel the paper he had taken from Morgan. \"I want you to take a good look at this paper, Mr. Pensel. Do you know if the Roman Theatre has such paper?\" [586] The manager gave the paper a strange look. \"No, I don't think we have such paper. In fact, I'm quite sure. What does it say?\" He turned around and called out, \"Nelson! What is this?\u2014Is it your latest promotional gimmick?\" He waved the paper in front of Nelson. [587] Nelson took the paper from the manager and quickly read it. \"Ah, it seems I'll have to change careers,\" he whispered. \"This is bound to break the advertising record.\" He read it again, his face showing envy. Then, under the gaze of four reproachful eyes, he handed the paper back to Pencier. \"I'm sorry, such a brilliant idea didn't even occur to me,\" he said slowly, retreating to the corner and crossing his arms. [588] The manager turned to Quine with a puzzled expression. \"This is strange, officer. As far as I know, the Roman Theater never uses such stationery, and I'm quite certain I never authorized anyone to do such promotional work. If Nelson says he didn't do it\u2014\"\n[589] Quine carefully put the paper in his pocket. \"Alright, thank you, gentlemen,\" he said, nodding at Pencier and Nelson as they left. [590] He looked at Morgan, who was flushed with anger and embarrassment. Quine raised his hand and slammed it lightly on the table again. [591] \"What do you think of this, Mr. Morgan?\" He asked straightforwardly. [592] Morgan jumped up. \"Damn it, this is a frame-up!\" He waved his fist in front of Quinn, \"Please excuse my rudeness, I know no more about this than you do. And don't think you can scare me by searching my hat and gloves - oh, you haven't searched my underwear yet, Inspector!\" [593] \"But my dear Morgan,\" Inspector Quinn said gently, \"why do you have to make yourself so nervous? Others would think I accused you of murdering Monte Field. Sit down, calm down, old boy. I'll ask you a simple question.\" [594] Morgan slumped in the chair, wiped his forehead with a trembling hand, and whispered, \"Sorry, Inspector, I couldn't control my temper. But these unfortunate things -\" He calmed down and murmured. [595] Inspector Quinn sat there, staring at him with a puzzled look. Morgan was busy wiping his sweat with a handkerchief and puffing on a cigar. Johnson coughed disapprovingly and looked up at the ceiling. Suddenly, there was a commotion in the room, but it soon quieted down. [596] Quin's voice broke the silence. \"Well, that's all, Mr. Morgan. You can go now.\" [597] Morgan slowly rose, opened his mouth to say something, but closed it again, put on his hat, and left the room. Johnson saw the officer's gesture and lazily stood up, casually opening the door for him. Both men left. [598] The room was left with Quin alone, who immediately got to work. He pulled out four ticket stubs, the letter Morgan had given him, and the lady's rhinestone handbag he had found in the dead man's pocket. He opened the handbag again, took out its contents, and laid them on the table in front of him. There were several business cards with \"Frances Eves-Popp\" printed elegantly; two delicate handkerchiefs; a small makeup case containing foundation, blush, and lipstick; a small money pouch containing twenty-dollar bills and some coins; and a door key. Quin toyed with these items for a moment, put them back in the handbag, and put the handbag, ticket stubs, and letter back in his pocket. He stood up, slowly scanning the room, then walked over to the coat rack, picked up a top hat hanging there, and checked its lining. It had the initials \"L.P.\" printed on it, and the hat size was 6 3/4, which piqued his interest. [599] He put the hat back in its place, opened the door. [600] The four people sitting in the reception room immediately stood up, their faces expressing relief. Quin stood at the door, smiling, with his hands in his coat pockets. [601] \"It's finally your turn,\" he said. \"Come on, everyone, come into the office.\" [602] He politely stepped aside to let them go first \u2013 three ladies and a young man. They all entered the office with some excitement, the young man hurrying to pull chairs for the ladies to sit down. Four pairs of eyes eagerly looked at the elderly man standing at the door. Quein smiled, like a fatherly figure. He quickly glanced at the reception room, then closed the door, and sternly walked to the table and sat down, reaching for his snuffbox. [603] \"Alright!\" he said mildly. \"I'm very sorry to have kept you waiting so long \u2013 official business, you know. Now, let's get started. Well, right, right, I must \u2013 well, now, first of all, ladies and this gentleman, how shall we begin? I believe, young lady, although I haven't had the pleasure of hearing you introduce yourself, your name should be Frances Eves-Pope, is that right?\" [604] The girl raised her eyebrows. \"Yes, sir,\" her voice was lively and melodious, \"although I'm not sure how you know my name.\" [605] She smiled. Her smile was attractive and seductive, and her gaze was captivating. She was in her prime, graceful and charming, with brown, large eyes and a complexion like jade. She exuded vitality that revitalized Quein's spirits. [606] He smiled at her. \"Well, Miss Eves-Pope, I think for most people, your identity would be a mystery. But I'm a policeman, so I'm stronger than most people, so it's not difficult for me to know your name. And you're definitely not an unknown girl, in fact, I saw your photo in the social section of the newspaper today.\" [607] The girl smiled, a little nervous. \"Oh, so that's how it is!\" She said, \"I'm starting to get scared. Sir, what do you want with me?\" [608] \"Official business - always official business,\" Detective Quin said pitifully, \"I'm interested in anyone, I always relate it to my profession... before asking you questions, I want to ask, who are your friends?\" [609] When Detective Quin looked at the three of them, all three coughed awkwardly. Miss Francis smiled charmingly. \"I apologize, sir, please allow me to introduce my friends. These two ladies are Hilda Orangi and Eve Ellis, my good friends; this is Mr. Stephen Barry, my fianc\u00e9.\" [610] Detective Quin looked at them with a touch of surprise. \"If I'm not mistaken, you're all members of the cast of 'Gunfight', right?\" [611] All three nodded in unison. [612] Queene turned to Francis. \"I don't mean to pry, Miss Eves-Pope, but I want you to explain... why your friends are here with you?\" [613] The three actors stood up very awkwardly. Francis looked from her companions to Queene, pleading with her eyes. [614] \"I\u2014I'm sorry, officer,\" she said quickly. \"I... I've never been questioned by the police before. I'm nervous, and I want my fianc\u00e9 and these two ladies\u2014my closest friends\u2014to be here with me when you ask me questions. I didn't realize it went against your wishes...\u201d\n[615] \"I understand,\" said Queene, smiling. \"I completely understand. But, you see\u2014\" he made a gesture for them to go out first. [616] Stephen Barry leaned over the girl's chair, a challenging look in his eyes as he stared at Quinn. \"Darling, I won't leave if you need me.\" [617] \"But, Stephen, dear\u2014\" Frances helplessly sobbed. Quinn's expression remained firm, showing no signs of relenting. \"You...you should go out first. But please wait outside for me. It won't be long, right, sir?\" she asked, her eyes showing displeasure. [618] Quinn shook his head. \"Not too long.\" His attitude had changed completely, seeming to become somewhat irritable. The other three seemed to sense his change, subtly aligning themselves against him. [619] Hilda Orlandi was a tall, plump woman in her forties, still attractive despite her age, with traces of her youthful beauty on her face. Under the cold light in the room, her face lost its color. She leaned over Frances, staring at Detective Quinn with a firm tone in her voice. \"We'll wait for you outside, dear, if you feel uncomfortable or anything, just call out, and we'll come right in to help you.\" She huffed out of the room, followed by Eve Ellis, who patted Frances' hand comfortingly. \"Don't worry, Frances, we're all here for you.\" She tugged on Barry's arm and followed Hilda Orlandi out. Barry glanced back at Quinn, a mix of anger and concern in his eyes. He glared at Quinn again before slamming the door shut. [620] Quinn immediately stood up, his movements clean and his attitude cold. He stared into Frances' eyes, his hand resting on the surface of the table. \"Now, Frances Eves-Pope, Miss,\" he said succinctly, \"I'd like to talk to you about this...\u201d He reached into his pocket, pulling out a rhinestone purse like a stage magician. \"I'd like to return your purse to you.\" [621] Francis sat up a little, her gaze shifting from Detective Quinn to the sparkling handbag. Her face turned pale. \"Oh, that's... that's my evening bag!\" [622] \"Indeed, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn said, \"it was found at the theater\u2014tonight.\" [623] \"Of course!\" She smiled nervously and sat back down. \"I'm such a fool! I didn't even know my bag was missing until now...\"\n[624] \"But, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Detective Quinn continued solemnly, \"where we found your bag is more important than finding the bag itself.\" [625] Francis opened her mouth wide, staring at him with fear in her eyes. \"Yes, I heard about it.\" She whispered. [626] \"Well, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn coldly continued, \"your bag was found in the pocket of the deceased!\" [627] Fear filled the girl's eyes. She let out a sob and swayed on her chair, her face pale and tense. [628] Quinn leaped forward, a look of pity and concern on his face. He touched Francis's weak body just as the door burst open and Stephen Barry rushed into the room, his coat flapping behind him. Hilda Orlancy, Eve Ellis, and Johnson followed close behind. [629] \"What did you do to her, you damned detective!\" Barry shouted, shoving Quinn aside. He gently held Francis in his arms, brushing aside the strand of black hair that was in front of her face and whispering comforting words in her ear. Francis sighed and looked up at the agitated face next to her, confused. \"Stephen, I...I feel dizzy,\" she murmured, and then sank back into Stephen's embrace. [630] \"Bring some water!\" the young man exclaimed, stroking her hand. Johnson immediately handed over a flat glass cup. Barry forced a few gulps down her throat, and Francis coughed and regained her senses. The two actresses pushed Barry aside and sternly ushered the men out of the room. Quinn followed the protesting young man and the detective out the door with a submissive expression. [631] \"You're a good cop, aren't you?\" Barry sniped at Quinny. \"What did you do to her \u2013 give her a whack on the head with your usual police tactics?\" [632] \"Oh, oh, young man,\" Quinny said mildly, \"don't talk so harshly. She was just frightened.\" [633] They all stood there in silence, the tension palpable. The door opened, and the two actresses emerged, supporting Francis. Barry rushed to her side, stroking her hand and whispering, \"Do you feel better now, dear?\" [634] \"Stephen \u2013 take me \u2013 home,\" she gasped, leaning on Barry. [635] Quinny stepped aside, letting them pass. He watched them slowly make their way to the front door, accompanied by a small crowd of spectators. [636] Chapter Six: The District Attorney Becomes a Biographer\n[637] Detective Richard Quinn was a unique man. Although small in stature, he was remarkably sturdy; his head was full of white hair, and his face was etched with lines that spoke of his rich life experiences.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."